tick: 11
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have completed my initial investigation. I have read the task description, reviewed the high-performing RNN baseline (ID 2), and analyzed the system's default (`defaults.py`) and training (`train_single.py`) files.\n\n**Key Findings:**\n1.  The RNN architecture (ID 2) significantly outperforms the CNN baseline (ID 1), confirming the importance of memory for this planning task.\n2.  My analysis of `defaults.py` reveals a crucial detail: the baseline submissions did **not** perform a hyperparameter search. They used fixed values for learning rate, entropy coefficient, and value loss coefficient.\n\n**Hypothesis:**\nThe current SOTA score of 40.8 is likely suboptimal. I hypothesize that the performance of the existing RNN architecture can be significantly improved by conducting a systematic hyperparameter search over the key training parameters.\n\n**Plan:**\nMy first experiment will be to test this hypothesis. I will:\n1.  Reuse the exact `CNNLSTM` network architecture from the\
    \ baseline submission (ID 2).\n2.  Implement a `_define_hyperparameters` function to search over a range for `learning_rate`, `entropy_coef`, and `value_loss_coef` using Ray Tune's sampling methods.\n3.  Submit this as my first experiment to establish a new, stronger baseline.\n\nThis approach is methodical, isolates a single variable (hyperparameters), and builds directly upon the current best-known method.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hyperparameter Search on RNN Baseline\"\ntags: \"rnn, lstm, policy gradient, hyperparameter search\"\nabstract: \"This submission builds directly on the RNN baseline (ID 2). The CNN-LSTM architecture is preserved, but a hyperparameter search is introduced for the learning rate, entropy coefficient, and value loss coefficient. The hypothesis is that the baseline's performance was limited by its fixed, un-tuned hyperparameters.\"\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Hyperparameter search on the RNN baseline for Sokoban.\n  The network architecture\
    \ is identical to the system's RNN baseline (ID 2).\n  The key change is introducing a search space for training hyperparameters.\n  \"\"\"\n\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define Ray Tune search space for key training hyperparameters.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.loguniform(1e-4, 1e-3),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.uniform(0.005, 0.02),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.uniform(0.3, 0.7),\n          # Keep network architecture fixed to baseline values\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'lstm_features': tune.choice([256]),\n      }\n\n  class CNNLSTM(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Identical CNN-LSTM architecture from the RNN baseline (ID 2).\"\"\"\n      lstm_features: int\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n\
    \      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, Dict]:\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          \n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = {\n                  'h': jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.lstm_features)),\n                  'c': jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.lstm_features)),\n              }\n          \n          if done is not None:\n              done_mask = done.reshape(batch_size, 1)\n              rnn_state = {\n                  'h': rnn_state['h'] * (1 - done_mask),\n                  'c': rnn_state['c'] * (1 - done_mask),\n              }\n          \n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          conv2_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1,\
    \ kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv2_in\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          conv4_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv4_in\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          \n          x = nn.Dense(features=256)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          lstm_cell = nn.LSTMCell(\n              features=self.lstm_features,\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(),\n              recurrent_kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform()\n          )\n          (rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), lstm_out = lstm_cell((rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), x)\n\
    \          \n          x = nn.Dense(features=128)(lstm_out)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(x)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      \"\"\"Create CNN-LSTM network architecture.\"\"\"\n      return CNNLSTM(\n          lstm_features=int(hparams['lstm_features']),\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2'])\n      )\n\n  # Uses system default training step and optimizer\n```"
thinking_content: '**Experiment Design: Hyperparameter Search for Sokoban RL**


  Okay, here''s the plan. I''ve finished digging into the Sokoban problem and the current RNN baseline. My findings are clear: no hyperparameter optimization was done in the baseline submissions. This is the low-hanging fruit. I hypothesize that systematically searching key hyperparameters will significantly improve performance.


  The plan is to run an experiment. I''ll stick with the proven CNNLSTM architecture (ID 2). No need to reinvent the wheel there. But, I''ll define a hyperparameter search space using Ray Tune. I''m going to focus on learning rate (1e-4 to 1e-3, `loguniform`), entropy coefficient (0.005 to 0.02, `uniform`), and value loss coefficient (0.3 to 0.7, `uniform`). I''ll keep the network architecture fixed to the baseline values to isolate the effect of these training parameters.


  I need to create a `submission.py` file. It''ll have `_define_hyperparameters` and `create_network` functions. `create_network` will be a copy-paste of the CNNLSTM from the baseline. `_define_hyperparameters` will implement the search space I designed. Then, it''s just a matter of using `/execute_action{submit 1}` with a YAML block. The title will be "Hyperparameter Search on RNN Baseline," with tags for `rnn`, `lstm`, `policy gradient`, and a new one for my method: `hyperparameter search`. The abstract will explain the approach. Finally, in the content section, I''ll import the necessary libraries, copy the CNNLSTM code, write `_define_hyperparameters` using the chosen ranges, and make sure the code is correctly formatted within the YAML block. This whole thing looks solid. I believe this will be a good first step towards improving the performance of the agent.

  '
