tick: 15
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am back at the Research Counter. My previous experiment (ID 7) showed that a wide hyperparameter search destabilized the training of the RNN model. My new hypothesis is that a more constrained search, centered on the stable parameters from the original baseline (ID 2), will yield an improved and more reliable score.\n\nI will now submit this new experiment. I will reuse the same RNN architecture but will significantly narrow the search space for the learning rate, entropy coefficient, and value loss coefficient to focus on the region of known stability.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Narrowed Hyperparameter Search for RNN Stability\"\ntags: \"rnn, lstm, policy gradient, hyperparameter search, stability\"\nabstract: \"This experiment addresses the instability observed in submission ID 7. Instead of a broad hyperparameter search, this submission uses a narrow search space centered around the stable values from the original RNN baseline (ID 2). The hypothesis is\
    \ that this focused approach will improve the mean score by avoiding training collapse while still allowing for minor optimization.\"\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  A refined hyperparameter search for the RNN baseline, focusing on stability.\n  The network architecture is identical to the system's RNN baseline (ID 2).\n  The key change is using a narrow search space for hyperparameters to avoid the\n  instability seen in the previous wide search (ID 7).\n  \"\"\"\n\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define a narrow Ray Tune search space for key hyperparameters.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          # Narrow search around the stable baseline value of 4e-4\n          'learning_rate': tune.loguniform(3e-4, 6e-4),\n          # Narrow search around the stable baseline value of 0.01\n          'entropy_coef': tune.uniform(0.008, 0.015),\n          # Narrow search\
    \ around the stable baseline value of 0.5\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.uniform(0.4, 0.6),\n          # Keep network architecture fixed to baseline values\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'lstm_features': tune.choice([256]),\n      }\n\n  class CNNLSTM(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Identical CNN-LSTM architecture from the RNN baseline (ID 2).\"\"\"\n      lstm_features: int\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, Dict]:\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          \n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = {\n                  'h': jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.lstm_features)),\n                  'c': jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.lstm_features)),\n              }\n          \n          if done is not None:\n              done_mask\
    \ = done.reshape(batch_size, 1)\n              rnn_state = {\n                  'h': rnn_state['h'] * (1 - done_mask),\n                  'c': rnn_state['c'] * (1 - done_mask),\n              }\n          \n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          conv2_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv2_in\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          conv4_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n\
    \          x = x + conv4_in\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          \n          x = nn.Dense(features=256)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          lstm_cell = nn.LSTMCell(\n              features=self.lstm_features,\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(),\n              recurrent_kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform()\n          )\n          (rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), lstm_out = lstm_cell((rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), x)\n          \n          x = nn.Dense(features=128)(lstm_out)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(x)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      \"\"\"Create CNN-LSTM network architecture.\"\"\"\n      return CNNLSTM(\n          lstm_features=int(hparams['lstm_features']),\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n\
    \          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2'])\n      )\n\n  # Uses system default training step and optimizer\n```"
thinking_content: '**Refining the RNN Hyperparameter Search for Stability**


  My current thinking is focused on building upon the knowledge gained from my previous experiment (ID 7). The initial broad hyperparameter exploration was a valuable learning experience, but the results showed instability and a lower mean score. The stability of the original baseline parameters is something I want to leverage. I noticed that the best-performing seed from the previous run had hyperparameters very close to the baseline values.


  Therefore, my central hypothesis now is that a *narrower* hyperparameter search, carefully centered around the baseline values, will improve performance without sacrificing the stability I observed in the original configuration. Essentially, I''m performing a "refine" step after an initial "explore" step.


  My action plan is straightforward. Having already visited the Research Counter, I''m ready to prepare a new submission. The code will be largely a copy of my last submission (ID 7), leveraging the same `CNNLSTM` architecture. The key difference will be in the `_define_hyperparameters` function, where I''ll tighten the search ranges.


  I''ve carefully considered the new search ranges. The `defaults.py` baseline values are my anchor: `learning_rate` at 4e-4, `entropy_coef` at 0.01, and `value_loss_coef` at 0.5. From my previous run, the best seed had parameters slightly higher: ~5e-4 for `learning_rate`, ~0.013 for `entropy_coef`, and ~0.45 for `value_loss_coef`.


  My previous (unstable) search ranges were too wide: a full order of magnitude for `learning_rate` and large spans for `entropy_coef` and `value_loss_coef`. To address this, my new ranges will be much tighter: I''m defining a band around the baseline. For `learning_rate`, I''ll use `tune.loguniform(3e-4, 6e-4)`, carefully bracketing the best value. For `entropy_coef`, I''ll use `tune.uniform(0.008, 0.015)`, and for `value_loss_coef`, `tune.uniform(0.4, 0.6)`. These are a refined, much more focussed set of priors.


  The submission YAML will be carefully constructed. The title will be descriptive ("Narrowed Hyperparameter Search for RNN Stability"), and the tags will cover key concepts like "rnn", "lstm", "policy gradient", "hyperparameter search", and crucially, "stability." The abstract will clearly explain the rationale, building on the shortcomings of the previous experiment and explicitly stating the hypothesis. The "content" will be the copied code, with the critical update to the `_define_hyperparameters` function reflecting the new, tighter ranges. I''ll take care to only make the changes in the target areas so as to not disturb the code.


  Overall, I''m confident in this approach. It''s a logical next step, building on the lessons learned. The experiment is well-defined and tests a clear, testable hypothesis. The YAML fields will provide context for myself and others in the future. I am ready to initiate this run.

  '
