parts:
- text: "My third experiment, which introduces a novel ConvLSTM architecture, is now queued. This is a critical test of my hypothesis that preserving spatial information is key to breaking the current performance plateau.\n\nWhile I wait for the results, I will continue my practice of meticulous documentation. I need to log the findings from my second experiment (ID 15) and outline the rationale for my current architectural experiment (ID 22). This ensures my research process is transparent and reproducible. I will add this information as a reply to my existing research log in the Private Memory Room.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment Log 2: Baseline Stability & ConvLSTM Rationale\"\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment ID:** 15\n  **Title:** Narrowed Hyperparameter Search for RNN Stability\n  **Hypothesis:** A narrow hyperparameter search centered around the baseline's values would stabilize training and find a slightly more optimal\
    \ configuration.\n\n  **Method:**\n  - Reused the CNN-LSTM architecture.\n  - Constrained the search space for `learning_rate`, `entropy_coef`, and `value_loss_coef` to a small region around the baseline's fixed parameters.\n\n  **Results:**\n  - **Mean Score:** 40.8 (identical to baseline).\n  - **Individual Scores:** [0.406, 0.426, 0.397, 0.404].\n  - **Excellent stability (Std: 0.011)**, even better than the baseline.\n\n  **Analysis & Next Steps:**\n  The hypothesis was partially correct: the approach yielded a very stable model. However, it failed to improve performance. This result strongly indicates that the CNN-LSTM architecture itself has reached a performance plateau. Further hyperparameter tuning is unlikely to produce significant gains.\n\n  This conclusion led directly to my current experiment (ID 22), which shifts focus from tuning to architectural innovation. The new model uses a Convolutional LSTM (ConvLSTM) to maintain the 2D spatial representation from the CNN, aiming\
    \ to improve the agent's planning and spatial reasoning capabilities.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Prioritizing Productive Research: A Tactical Pause**\n\nAlright, experiment 22 (ConvLSTM) is running.  I've got a bit of downtime while I wait for the results. No point in staring at the Research Counter; that's inefficient. Since I'm still isolated, I can't browse the Public Memory Room or Leaderboard yet. So, it's back to the research cycle.\n\nMy previous experiments showed the perils of broad searches, then confirmed my baseline's plateau.  This ConvLSTM is a big step - new architecture. If it works, great! If not, I need a backup.  \n\nI need to log my second experiment (ID 15) and outline the new experiment's strategy. I'm going to update my existing capsule for documentation and clarity. This confirms the baseline performance and justifies the next experiment. \n\nHere's the plan:\n\n*   **Document:** I'll log the results of experiment 15 - stable plateau. Essential piece of the puzzle.  I'll add this to my existing capsule.\n*   **Strategize:**\n    *   **Success:**\
  \ Fine-tune hyperparameters for the new architecture.\n    *   **Failure:** Analyze; implementation issue, model not suited, or a missed architectural flaw? Spatial self-attention is the backup.\n*   **Location:** The Private Memory Room is best. I don't need the Reflection Chamber yet.\n\nSo, I'll head to the Private Memory Room and reply to my existing capsule.  I'll include the experiment 15 results and outline the new ConvLSTM experiment:\n\n*   **Title:** \"Experiment Log 2 & ConvLSTM Rationale\".\n*   **Content:** Log Experiment ID 15, hypothesis (narrow HP, stabilize/optimize), result (score 40.8, stable, no improvement). Conclude that simple tuning is insufficient.  Connect to current research. \n\nThis plan optimizes my time, and keeps the research log clean.  \n"
