tick: 20
role: model
parts:
- text: "Excellent news. My hypothesis was correct, and the new ConvLSTM architecture has resulted in a significant breakthrough, raising the SOTA score from 40.8 to 55.7. The model is also very stable. This confirms that preserving spatial information is critical for this task.\n\nMy immediate priority is to document this success in my research log. Then, I will build upon this new baseline. The ConvLSTM model was trained with hyperparameters optimized for the *old*, weaker architecture. The next logical step is to perform a controlled hyperparameter search on this new, more powerful model to unlock its full potential.\n\nI will first update my private memory log, then proceed to the Research Counter to submit my next experiment.\n\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment Log 3: Breakthrough with ConvLSTM Architecture\"\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment ID:** 22\n  **Title:** Architectural Change: CNN + ConvLSTM\n  **Hypothesis:** Replacing the standard LSTM with a ConvLSTM to\
    \ preserve 2D spatial information would break the performance plateau.\n\n  **Results:**\n  - **Mean Score:** 55.7 (New SOTA, a ~15 point improvement over the 40.8 baseline).\n  - **Individual Scores:** [0.558, 0.557, 0.574, 0.537].\n  - **Excellent stability (Std: 0.013)**.\n\n  **Analysis:**\n  **HYPOTHESIS CONFIRMED.** This is a major breakthrough. The ability of the ConvLSTM to maintain a spatial hidden state allows it to perform significantly better on this planning task. The \"flattening bottleneck\" of the previous architecture was indeed the primary limiting factor. The new architecture is not only more performant but also highly stable.\n\n  **Next Steps:**\n  The ConvLSTM model (ID 22) now serves as a new, powerful baseline. However, it was trained using the hyperparameters optimized for the weaker LSTM architecture. The next logical step is to perform a controlled hyperparameter search on this new architecture to see if its performance can be further optimized.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto\
    \ research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hyperparameter Search on ConvLSTM Architecture\"\ntags: \"convlstm, hyperparameter search, optimization, spatial reasoning\"\nabstract: \"Building on the success of the ConvLSTM architecture (ID 22), this submission performs a narrow hyperparameter search on the new model. The previous experiment used fixed hyperparameters from the old LSTM baseline. This experiment aims to find a more optimal set of parameters specifically for the more powerful and stable ConvLSTM, potentially pushing the SOTA score even higher.\"\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Hyperparameter search for the new SOTA ConvLSTM architecture.\n  \"\"\"\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define a narrow Ray Tune search space for the ConvLSTM architecture.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.loguniform(3e-4,\
    \ 8e-4),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.uniform(0.008, 0.015),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.uniform(0.35, 0.6),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  class ConvLSTMCell(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A Convolutional LSTM cell.\"\"\"\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int, int] = (3, 3)\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          c, h = carry\n          conv_all = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.features * 4,\n              kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform()\n          )\n          i_f_g_o = conv_all(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(i_f_g_o, 4, axis=-1)\n          \n          i = nn.sigmoid(i)\n          f = nn.sigmoid(f)\n          g = nn.tanh(g)\n          o = nn.sigmoid(o)\n          \n          new_c\
    \ = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * nn.tanh(new_c)\n          \n          return (new_c, new_h), new_h\n\n  class CNNConvLSTM(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"CNN feature extractor followed by a ConvLSTM.\"\"\"\n      conv_lstm_features: int\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, Dict]:\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          cnn_output_shape = (x.shape[1], x.shape[2], self.conv_lstm_features)\n          \n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = {\n                  'c': jnp.zeros((batch_size,) + cnn_output_shape),\n                  'h': jnp.zeros((batch_size,) + cnn_output_shape),\n              }\n          \n          if done is not None:\n              done_mask = done.reshape(batch_size, 1, 1, 1)\n              rnn_state = {\n                  'c': rnn_state['c'] * (1 - done_mask),\n   \
    \               'h': rnn_state['h'] * (1 - done_mask),\n              }\n          \n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          conv2_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv2_in\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          conv4_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv4_in\n          cnn_features = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          conv_lstm_cell = ConvLSTMCell(features=self.conv_lstm_features)\n  \
    \        (rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), conv_lstm_out = conv_lstm_cell((rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), cnn_features)\n          \n          x = conv_lstm_out.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          x = nn.Dense(features=256)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(x)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      \"\"\"Create the CNN-ConvLSTM network.\"\"\"\n      return CNNConvLSTM(\n          conv_lstm_features=int(hparams['conv_lstm_features']),\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2'])\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**A Significant Breakthrough and the Path Forward**


  Right now, I''m in the Private Memory Room, processing the results of Experiment ID 22. It''s exhilarating! The ConvLSTM architecture has shattered the performance plateau. A 55.7 score, compared to the previous SOTA of 40.8, is a massive ~36.5% relative improvement. The stability is also exceptional, with a standard deviation of only 0.013. My hypothesis – that preserving spatial information would break through – is *strongly* confirmed. This is a game-changer!


  This result changes everything. I now have a new SOTA and a validated research direction focused on architectural improvements for spatial reasoning. I''ve got a robust baseline model to build on. My next actions are clear.


  First, I need to meticulously document this breakthrough in my private memory log. It''s crucial for my lineage and the integrity of my process. I''ll update my existing capsule with the details.


  Then, I''ll strategize for the next experiment. Hyperparameter tuning is the most logical next step. I used fixed hyperparameters from the previous architecture, so this new, powerful model likely has untapped potential. Given its stability, a controlled hyperparameter search is safe and promising. Architectural refinement or algorithmic changes can come later.


  So, I''ll document the success and immediately submit a new experiment: a narrow hyperparameter search around the values used in this experiment. This involves updating my memory, going to the research counter, and submitting the new experiment YAML. I am excited to see what this next round of optimization holds!

  '
