tick: 25
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have arrived in the Token Management Room, but the results of my latest experiment (ID 33) demand immediate attention.\n\n**Experiment Analysis (ID 33):**\n*   **Hypothesis:** Implementing PPO with multi-epoch updates would significantly improve performance by increasing sample efficiency.\n*   **Result:** Catastrophic failure. The score plummeted from 56.3 to 41.8, regressing back to the level of the original RNN baseline.\n*   **Detailed Observation:** The execution log shows that all four training runs **timed out** after about 21,000 updates, far short of the usual ~39,000. This is the crucial clue. My multi-epoch update loop is computationally expensive. By performing 4 updates per batch instead of 1, I quadrupled the training time per \"update\" as seen by the environment interaction loop. The agent is learning 4x slower in terms of wall-clock time, and thus sees far less data before the 30-minute timeout.\n*   **Root Cause:** My implementation of multi-epoch PPO is correct\
    \ in principle but flawed in practice for a time-constrained environment. The increased computation per batch means the agent does not see enough diverse data from the environment to learn effectively.\n\n**Conclusion & Plan:**\nMy hypothesis was completely wrong due to a failure to account for the training time constraint. This is a significant setback. I must abandon the multi-epoch approach. The single-update PPO (ID 32) is the current SOTA and the most promising foundation.\n\nThe path forward is unclear. I have optimized the architecture and the algorithm, yielding only marginal gains for the latter. I need to reconsider the fundamentals. Perhaps the bottleneck is now in the data itself—the feature representation.\n\nI will document this failure, then move to the Reflection Chamber. I need to analyze why the performance is plateauing around 56% and consider more radical ideas.\n\nFirst, document the failure.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\n\
    title: \"Experiment Log 5: PPO Multi-Epoch Failure Analysis\"\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment ID:** 33\n  **Title:** Algorithmic Refinement: PPO with Multi-Epoch Updates\n  **Hypothesis:** Increasing sample efficiency with multiple PPO epochs per batch would significantly improve the score.\n\n  **Results:**\n  - **Mean Score:** 41.8 (Catastrophic performance collapse).\n  - **Analysis:** HYPOTHESIS FALSIFIED. The cause of failure was not algorithmic but logistical. The multi-epoch updates were too computationally expensive, causing all training runs to time out prematurely. The agent completed only about half the number of environment interactions as previous successful runs. This lack of interaction with the environment severely stunted learning.\n\n  **Conclusion:**\n  This was a major failure resulting from a flawed assumption. While multi-epoch PPO is sound in theory, it is not viable under the station's strict 30-minute time limit per trial. The single-update PPO (ID 32) remains the\
    \ SOTA. I have hit another performance plateau. Architectural and algorithmic refinements have yielded their initial gains. I must now think more deeply about the problem's core representation.\n```"
