- text: "My third attempt at the feature engineering experiment (ID 50) is now running. I have systematically addressed the bugs from the previous two failures, and I am confident that this version is robust. The scientific hypothesis—that enriching the input representation is the key to the next breakthrough—will now be properly tested.\n\nWhile the experiment is running, I must use my time productively. The station guidelines recommend publishing a paper roughly every 100 ticks. I am at tick 30 and have now conducted a series of experiments that tell a coherent story: from identifying the baseline's instability, to stabilizing it, to breaking its performance plateau with a new architecture (ConvLSTM), and finally to refining the training algorithm (PPO). This is a solid foundation for my first publication.\n\nI will begin drafting my first research paper in my Private Memory Room. This will allow me to consolidate my findings and prepare for publication once I reach maturity and gain access\
    \ to the Archive Room.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Draft: From Bottlenecks to Breakthroughs in Sokoban\"\ntags: \"publication draft, research summary, convlstm, ppo, feature engineering\"\nabstract: \"This capsule contains the draft of my first research paper. It documents the systematic process of identifying and overcoming performance bottlenecks in a reinforcement learning agent for Sokoban, starting from the baseline RNN and progressing through architectural and algorithmic innovations.\"\ncontent: |\n  # DRAFT: From Bottlenecks to Breakthroughs: A Systematic Approach to Improving Planning Agents for Sokoban\n\n  ## 1. Introduction\n  \n  This paper details a series of experiments aimed at improving the performance of a reinforcement learning agent on the planning-intensive task of Sokoban. Starting from the provided system baselines, we identified three primary bottlenecks limiting performance: training instability, architectural\
    \ representation, and algorithmic efficiency. Through a methodical process of hypothesis, experimentation, and analysis, we demonstrate a clear path of improvement, culminating in a novel agent architecture and training methodology that significantly surpasses the initial state-of-the-art.\n\n  ## 2. Initial Baseline Analysis (Experiments ID 7, 15)\n\n  The initial SOTA was an RNN-based agent (ID 2) with a score of 40.8. Our first experiment (ID 7) attempted a broad hyperparameter search, which resulted in a catastrophic performance collapse (score 31.5) due to high training instability. This falsified the initial hypothesis that simple tuning was sufficient. A subsequent, narrowed search (ID 15) successfully stabilized training but failed to improve the score, confirming that the baseline architecture itself had reached a performance plateau. This analysis concluded that a more fundamental change was required.\n\n  *(This draft will be expanded upon as further results become available.)*\n\
    ```"
thinking_content: "**Prioritizing & Planning: A Proactive Approach**\n\nOkay, so I've just submitted my third attempt at feature engineering, experiment ID 50, and I'm feeling cautiously optimistic. This version feels solid; I've systematically addressed the bugs that plagued the previous iterations by integrating the feature engineering directly into the model. Now, the experiment is running, and I'm back in the Research Counter with my token budget at 32% and a mental clock that reads 30 ticks.\n\nMy immediate instinct is to acknowledge this submission with high confidence. I've put a lot of work into this and addressed the major concerns I had during the previous submissions. Now, what to do while I wait? Idleness is not an option; the station practically frowns upon it.\n\nI've got two main branches of thought here:\n\n*   **Option 1:** Wait for the results. Efficient? No.\n*   **Option 2:** Preemptively plan the next experiment. \n    *   If v3 works, the logical step would be to add\
  \ more features, perhaps incorporating that box-target proximity I mused about earlier.\n    *   If it fails... I'll need a completely new direction.\n\nI could also think about other stations' features. I'm too \"immature\" (under 50 ticks) for the Public Memory Room or the Archive Room. There's the Mail Room, but I've no one to contact, and no reason to do so.\n\nI've been working intensely. The Reflection Chamber might be a good call here. It's time to consolidate my thoughts. My last reflection was about *what* to do next. Perhaps this one could be about *how* I'm doing it. My last three submissions have involved bug fixes, so: \"How can I improve my development process to catch these errors before submission?\"\n\nAnd then it hit me: I need to think about my first paper. The guidance says \"at least one paper every 100 ticks\", and I'm at tick 30. A paper needs a critical mass of experiments to back it up (5+) and I've got a growing archive:\n*   ID 7: Wide HParam search (fail)\n\
  *   ID 15: Narrow HParam search (success, reproduced baseline)\n*   ID 22: ConvLSTM (success, new SOTA)\n*   ID 27: HParam on ConvLSTM (marginal fail)\n*   ID 32: PPO (success, marginal SOTA)\n*   ID 33: Multi-epoch PPO (fail)\n*   ID 44: Feature Eng v1 (fail)\n*   ID 46: Feature Eng v2 (fail)\n*   ID 50: Feature Eng v3 (running)\n\nI've got the narrative arc for a solid paper. From the basic RNN to my SOTA ConvLSTM + PPO, including my failures, and what I learned from them. It's now enough to start writing a paper.\n\nThe Archive Room is still off-limits until I reach 50 ticks, but I can *prepare* the paper. I'll head to my Private Memory Room and start drafting.\n\nMy plan crystallizes:\n\n1.  Acknowledge the submitted experiment (ID 50) and have high confidence\n2.  While I wait, I'll start drafting my first research paper. I've got enough content to fill it with.\n3.  Go to the Private Memory room. Create a new capsule for this draft.\n4.  Start writing the introduction and the baseline\
  \ analysis.\n\nThis feels like a productive use of my time, addressing one of my core goals, and it makes my next steps proactive.\n"
