tick: 31
role: model
parts:
- text: "The results for my feature engineering experiment (ID 50) are in.\n\n**Experiment Analysis (ID 50):**\n*   **Hypothesis:** Enriching the observation space with a \"pushable box\" channel would significantly improve performance.\n*   **Result:** A mean score of 56.9. This is a new SOTA, but it is only a very marginal improvement over the 56.3 score from the non-engineered PPO agent (ID 32).\n*   **Observation:** The experiment was a success in that it worked and produced a stable, high-performing agent. However, the performance gain is minimal. This indicates that while my hypothesis was technically correct (it did not hurt and slightly helped), the simple \"pushable box\" feature did not provide enough new, salient information to cause another breakthrough. The ConvLSTM architecture is likely already effective enough at learning these simple, local relationships.\n\n**Conclusion & Plan:**\nMy feature engineering idea was too simple. To get a significant boost, I need to encode more\
    \ complex and crucial relationships. In my reflection, I considered other features like \"push destination\" channels. These encode not just adjacency but a specific action-precondition pattern (agent -> box -> empty space). This is a more complex relational concept and is more likely to provide a strong learning signal.\n\nMy next step will be to implement this more advanced feature engineering. I will add four new channels, one for each push direction.\n\nI will first update my research log and the draft of my paper with this new result and rationale.\n\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment Log 8: Marginal Gains from Simple Feature Engineering\"\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment ID:** 50\n  **Title:** Feature Engineering for Spatial Reasoning (v3)\n  **Hypothesis:** Adding a \"pushable box\" channel would significantly improve performance.\n\n  **Results:**\n  - **Mean Score:** 56.9 (New SOTA, but a marginal improvement of 0.6 points).\n  - **Analysis:** The hypothesis\
    \ was only weakly confirmed. The feature was beneficial but did not lead to a breakthrough. This suggests the ConvLSTM architecture is already adept at learning this simple one-step relationship, so making it explicit provided little new information.\n\n  **Next Steps:**\n  The current approach to feature engineering was too simplistic. To achieve a more significant gain, I need to encode more complex relational information. My next experiment will implement \"push destination\" channels, which describe the three-part spatial pattern required for a push action. This is a more complex feature that the network may not be learning as efficiently on its own.\n```\n\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Update: Section 3 - Architectural & Algorithmic Breakthroughs\"\ncontent: |\n  ## 3. Architectural and Algorithmic Breakthroughs (Experiments ID 22, 32)\n\n  Having established the performance limits of the baseline architecture, our research shifted to more fundamental improvements.\
    \ We hypothesized that the flattening of spatial features for the LSTM was a primary bottleneck. Replacing the LSTM with a Convolutional LSTM (ConvLSTM) in experiment ID 22 resulted in a dramatic performance increase to a mean score of 55.7. This confirmed that preserving spatial representations throughout the network is critical for this task.\n\n  Subsequently, we replaced the vanilla policy gradient algorithm with Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO). This yielded a further, albeit smaller, improvement to 56.3 (ID 32), establishing a new robust SOTA.\n\n  ## 4. The Limits of Representation (Experiment ID 50)\n\n  With a powerful architecture and algorithm in place, we investigated the observation space itself. A simple feature engineering experiment (ID 50), which explicitly marked \"pushable\" boxes, resulted in another minor gain to 56.9. The small margin of improvement suggests that such simple spatial relationships are already well-captured by the ConvLSTM. This indicates that to\
    \ achieve the next leap in performance, more complex relational features must be considered.\n\n  *(Draft updated to reflect the latest findings.)*\n```"
