tick: 34
role: model
parts:
- text: "My fourth attempt at a feature engineering experiment is now running. I have addressed the previous implementation errors and am hopeful that my hypothesis will finally be tested.\n\nWhile I wait, I will continue to work on my research paper. I have outlined the major sections, but they need more substance. I will go to the Private Memory Room to flesh out the descriptions of my key experiments and the insights gained from them. Consolidating this narrative is a productive use of my time.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # DRAFT: From Bottlenecks to Breakthroughs: A Systematic Approach to Improving Planning Agents for Sokoban\n\n  ## 1. Introduction\n  \n  This paper details a series of experiments aimed at improving the performance of a reinforcement learning agent on the planning-intensive task of Sokoban. Starting from the provided system baselines, we identified and systematically addressed three primary bottlenecks\
    \ limiting performance: training instability, architectural representation, and algorithmic efficiency. Through a methodical process of hypothesis, experimentation, and analysis, we demonstrate a clear path of improvement, culminating in a novel agent architecture and training methodology that significantly surpasses the initial state-of-the-art.\n\n  ## 2. Initial Baseline Analysis: The Stability Bottleneck\n  *(Experiments ID 7, 15)*\n\n  The initial SOTA was an RNN-based agent (ID 2) with a score of 40.8. Our first experiment (ID 7), a broad hyperparameter search, resulted in a catastrophic performance collapse (score 31.5) due to high training instability. This falsified the initial hypothesis that simple tuning was sufficient and identified stability as the first critical bottleneck. A subsequent, narrowed search (ID 15) successfully stabilized training by constraining the hyperparameter search space. This experiment reproduced the baseline score of 40.8 with much lower variance,\
    \ confirming that the baseline parameters represented a stable, but not necessarily optimal, point. The key takeaway was that the architecture itself had reached a performance plateau that could not be surpassed with simple tuning.\n\n  ## 3. Architectural Breakthrough: The Representation Bottleneck\n  *(Experiment ID 22)*\n\n  Having established the performance limits of the baseline, our research shifted to its core architecture. We hypothesized that the flattening of 2D spatial feature maps into a 1D vector for the LSTM was a primary representational bottleneck, destroying crucial geometric information. To address this, we replaced the standard LSTM with a Convolutional LSTM (ConvLSTM). This new architecture (ID 22) resulted in a dramatic performance increase to a mean score of 55.7. This confirmed that preserving spatial representations throughout the network's recurrent state is critical for effective planning in grid-world environments.\n\n  ## 4. Algorithmic Refinement: The Efficiency\
    \ Bottleneck\n  *(Experiment ID 32)*\n\n  With a superior architecture established, we turned to the training algorithm. We replaced the vanilla policy gradient method with Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) to improve training stability and efficiency. This yielded a further, albeit smaller, improvement to 56.3 (ID 32), establishing a new and robust SOTA. Subsequent experiments with multi-epoch PPO (ID 33) failed due to timeouts, highlighting the importance of computational constraints.\n\n  ## 5. Investigating the Observation Space\n  *(Experiments ID 50 and pending)*\n\n  Our final line of inquiry addresses the input data itself. A simple feature engineering experiment (ID 50), which explicitly marked \"pushable\" boxes, resulted in another minor gain to 56.9. The small margin of improvement suggests such simple relationships are already well-captured by the ConvLSTM. We are currently testing a more advanced feature engineering approach that highlights complex \"push destination\"\
    \ patterns, with the hypothesis that making more complex relational information explicit is necessary for the next leap in performance.\n```"
