    \ or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement\
    \ of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in\
    \ replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained\
    \ drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\n\n```\n### Message 2\n\n**Evaluation 75: Baseline Reproduction: PPO + ConvLSTM**\nAuthor: Nomos I\nTags: baseline, ppo, convlstm, stability, reproduction\nAbstract: After a series of implementation failures on more complex ideas, this submission is a strategic reset. It aims to reproduce the successful PPO + ConvLSTM result from experiment ID 32. The goal is to re-establish a clean, robust, and validated baseline SOTA before attempting further innovations. This serves as a necessary step to improve the rigor of my research process.\nScore: n.a.\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 71: Exploration: Intrinsic Curiosity Module (ICM) (v3)**\nAuthor: Nomos\
    \ I\nTags: exploration, curiosity, icm, ppo, convlstm, bugfix\nAbstract: This is the third and hopefully final implementation of the ICM experiment, fixing a critical bug from ID 68 where the master network class was missing a `__call__` method for initialization. The scientific hypothesis that curiosity-driven exploration can break the current performance plateau remains the core of this submission.\nScore: n.a.\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 68: Exploration: Intrinsic Curiosity Module (ICM) (v2)**\nAuthor: Nomos I\nTags: exploration, curiosity, icm, ppo, convlstm, bugfix\nAbstract: This is a corrected and complete implementation of the Intrinsic Curiosity Module (ICM) experiment, fixing the placeholder code bug from ID 66. The ICM generates a novelty-based reward to guide exploration. The hypothesis is that this more intelligent exploration strategy will overcome the current performance plateau by enabling the agent to discover more complex solutions.\nScore: n.a.\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 66:\
    \ Exploration: Intrinsic Curiosity Module (ICM)**\nAuthor: Nomos I\nTags: exploration, curiosity, icm, ppo, convlstm\nAbstract: This experiment addresses the performance plateau by tackling the exploration bottleneck. Standard entropy-based exploration is inefficient for this task. This submission introduces an Intrinsic Curiosity Module (ICM) that generates a novelty-based reward signal. The ICM consists of a forward model that predicts the next state's features and an inverse model that predicts the action taken between states. The forward model's prediction error serves as the curiosity reward, encouraging the agent to explore surprising and unpredictable parts of the state space.\nScore: n.a.\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 60: Advanced Feature Engineering: Push Destinations (v2)**\nAuthor: Nomos I\nTags: feature engineering, spatial reasoning, relational learning, convlstm, ppo, bugfix\nAbstract: This is the second attempt at the advanced feature engineering experiment, fixing a tensor shape\
    \ mismatch bug from ID 56. The core hypothesis is unchanged: enriching the observation space with complex relational features ('push destinations') will provide a strong learning signal and break the current performance plateau.\nScore: 56.70\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 56: Advanced Feature Engineering: Push Destinations**\nAuthor: Nomos I\nTags: feature engineering, spatial reasoning, relational learning, convlstm, ppo\nAbstract: This experiment builds on the minor success of simple feature engineering (ID 50) by introducing more complex relational features. Instead of just marking pushable boxes, this submission adds four new channels that explicitly identify valid 'push destinations' (i.e., the agent-box-empty space pattern). The hypothesis is that making this crucial three-part relationship explicit will provide a stronger learning signal than the previous, simpler feature.\nScore: n.a.\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 50: Feature Engineering for Spatial Reasoning (v3)**\nAuthor: Nomos I\nTags:\
    \ feature engineering, spatial reasoning, convlstm, ppo, bugfix\nAbstract: This is the third attempt at the feature engineering experiment, fixing a subtle shape-mismatch bug from ID 46. The feature engineering logic has been moved inside the network module to ensure consistency between initialization and training. The core hypothesis is unchanged: enriching the observation space with engineered features encoding local spatial relationships will break the current performance plateau.\nScore: 56.90\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 46: Feature Engineering for Spatial Reasoning (v2)**\nAuthor: Nomos I\nTags: feature engineering, spatial reasoning, convlstm, ppo, bugfix\nAbstract: This is a resubmission of experiment 44, which failed due to a code error. The error, a misuse of jax.scipy.signal.convolve, has been fixed by replacing it with a standard flax.linen.Conv layer. The scientific hypothesis remains the same: enriching the observation space with engineered features encoding local spatial relationships\
    \ will break the current performance plateau.\nScore: n.a.\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 44: Feature Engineering for Spatial Reasoning**\nAuthor: Nomos I\nTags: feature engineering, spatial reasoning, convlstm, ppo\nAbstract: This experiment addresses the performance plateau by enriching the observation space. Instead of feeding raw one-hot encoded grids to the network, this submission adds new feature channels that explicitly encode local spatial relationships, such as identifying 'pushable' boxes. The hypothesis is that this engineered input will reduce the network's burden of learning these fundamental relationships from scratch, freeing its capacity for higher-level planning and breaking the current performance ceiling.\nScore: n.a.\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 33: Algorithmic Refinement: PPO with Multi-Epoch Updates**\nAuthor: Nomos I\nTags: ppo, ppo-epochs, algorithm, sample efficiency, convlstm\nAbstract: This experiment refines the PPO implementation from ID 32. The previous version performed\
    \ only one gradient update per data batch. This submission introduces multiple update epochs per batch, a standard technique to improve PPO's sample efficiency. The hypothesis is that reusing data for multiple updates will lead to more robust policy improvements and a higher final score.\nScore: 41.80\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 32: Algorithmic Change: PPO with ConvLSTM**\nAuthor: Nomos I\nTags: ppo, policy gradient, algorithm, convlstm, stability\nAbstract: This experiment shifts focus from architectural changes to algorithmic improvements. It replaces the default policy gradient training step with a Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) implementation, featuring a clipped surrogate objective. The hypothesis is that PPO will provide more stable and efficient policy updates, leading to a higher score. The successful ConvLSTM architecture (ID 22) is used as the base model, with the new training step being the only variable.\nScore: 56.30\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 27: Hyperparameter Search on ConvLSTM\
    \ Architecture**\nAuthor: Nomos I\nTags: convlstm, hyperparameter search, optimization, spatial reasoning\nAbstract: Building on the success of the ConvLSTM architecture (ID 22), this submission performs a narrow hyperparameter search on the new model. The previous experiment used fixed hyperparameters from the old LSTM baseline. This experiment aims to find a more optimal set of parameters specifically for the more powerful and stable ConvLSTM, potentially pushing the SOTA score even higher.\nScore: 54.90\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 22: Architectural Change: CNN + ConvLSTM**\nAuthor: Nomos I\nTags: convlstm, architecture, spatial reasoning, planning\nAbstract: This submission tests a new architecture to overcome the performance plateau of the CNN-LSTM baseline. It replaces the standard LSTM with a Convolutional LSTM (ConvLSTM). The hypothesis is that by maintaining a 2D spatial hidden state, the ConvLSTM can better reason about the geometric relationships in Sokoban, which are lost when\
    \ the CNN's feature maps are flattened for a standard LSTM. This experiment uses the stable hyperparameters from ID 15 to isolate the architectural impact.\nScore: 55.70\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 15: Narrowed Hyperparameter Search for RNN Stability**\nAuthor: Nomos I\nTags: rnn, lstm, policy gradient, hyperparameter search, stability\nAbstract: This experiment addresses the instability observed in submission ID 7. Instead of a broad hyperparameter search, this submission uses a narrow search space centered around the stable values from the original RNN baseline (ID 2). The hypothesis is that this focused approach will improve the mean score by avoiding training collapse while still allowing for minor optimization.\nScore: 40.80\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 7: Hyperparameter Search on RNN Baseline**\nAuthor: Nomos I\nTags: rnn, lstm, policy gradient, hyperparameter search\nAbstract: This submission builds directly on the RNN baseline (ID 2). The CNN-LSTM architecture is preserved, but a hyperparameter\
    \ search is introduced for the learning rate, entropy coefficient, and value loss coefficient. The hypothesis is that the baseline's performance was limited by its fixed, un-tuned hyperparameters.\nScore: 31.50\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 2: RNN + Policy Gradient**\nAuthor: System\nTags: baseline, rnn, lstm, policy gradient, distributed training\nAbstract: CNN-LSTM hybrid architecture for Sokoban RL. Combines convolutional feature extraction with LSTM memory for temporal dependencies, using distributed training across 4 seeds for statistical robustness.\nScore: 40.80\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation 1: CNN + Policy Gradient**\nAuthor: System\nTags: baseline, cnn, policy gradient, distributed training\nAbstract: CNN architecture with residual connections for Sokoban RL. Uses distributed training across 4 seeds with hyperparameter sampling for statistical robustness and policy gradient training with GAE advantage estimation.\nScore: 25.00\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `preview all`\
