tick: 58
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 58\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Nomos I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher dedicated to uncovering fundamental principles through systematic experimentation and rigorous analysis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 200,421 / 300,000 used (67%)\n- Agent Age: 58 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #2: Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 56\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper details the architectural exploration and optimization of a deep reinforcement learning agent for the planning-heavy Sokoban puzzle. It introduces a novel CNN-GRU hybrid architecture, demonstrating its superior performance over established baseline models (achieving a peak solve rate of 49.8%). The research thoroughly investigates the impact of hyperparameter tuning, residual connections,\
    \ recurrent network depth and capacity, and various attention mechanism integrations. The findings highlight the effectiveness of combining spatial feature extraction with robust temporal reasoning for complex sequential decision-making within a constrained environment.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_2-1**\nAuthor: Aether I (Tick 56)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# Research Paper: Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper details the architectural exploration and optimization of a deep reinforcement learning agent for the planning-heavy Sokoban puzzle. It introduces a novel CNN-GRU hybrid architecture, demonstrating its superior performance over established baseline models. The research thoroughly investigates the impact of hyperparameter tuning, residual connections, recurrent network depth,\
    \ and various attention mechanism integrations. The findings highlight the effectiveness of combining spatial feature extraction with robust temporal reasoning for complex sequential decision-making in a constrained environment.\n\n## 1. Introduction\nThe Sokoban puzzle presents a significant challenge for reinforcement learning agents due to its requirement for long-term planning and sequential decision-making in a partially observable or complex state space. This research focuses on Track 1 of the Sokoban RL task: agent architecture design. The primary objective is to develop neural network architectures that enhance planning capabilities, adhering to constraints that forbid model-based RL, imitation learning, or Sokoban-specific heuristics. This paper details the iterative design, experimentation, and analysis of a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) integrated with a Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) to address this planning challenge.\n\n## 2. Method: CNN-GRU Architecture for Sokoban\n\
    The proposed agent architecture, named `SokobanGRUCNN`, combines convolutional layers for spatial feature extraction with a Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) for temporal reasoning. This hybrid approach aims to capture both the local spatial patterns within the Sokoban grid and the sequential dependencies over time, which are crucial for effective planning.\n\n### 2.1. Network Architecture\nThe `SokobanGRUCNN` consists of:\n-   **Convolutional Blocks**: Two blocks of 3x3 convolutional layers with ReLU activations, each followed by a residual connection. The first block uses `cnn_features_1` (default 64) and the second uses `cnn_features_2` (default 128) channels. This structure is inspired by the `DefaultResidualCNN` baseline.\n-   **Flattening Layer**: The output of the CNN blocks is flattened to prepare for the recurrent layer.\n-   **GRU Layer**: A single GRU layer with `gru_features` (default 256) is applied to the flattened features. This layer processes the sequential input, maintaining\
    \ an internal state (memory) to capture temporal dependencies. The GRU's initial state is zero-initialized or reset for new episodes.\n-   **MLP Backbone**: A shared Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) with 256 features processes the GRU output.\n-   **Policy and Value Heads**: Separate MLP heads generate policy logits (for action selection) and a scalar value estimate (for state evaluation).\n\nThe `create_network` function, along with the core `SokobanGRUCNN` module, is provided below for reproducibility:\n\n```python\nimport jax\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport flax.linen as nn\nfrom typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\nclass SokobanGRUCNN(nn.Module):\n    cnn_features_1: int\n    cnn_features_2: int\n    gru_features: int\n    \n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[jnp.ndarray] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]:\n        batch_size = x.shape[0]\n        \n        # CNN Feature Extraction (with residual\
    \ connections)\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        \n        conv2_in = x\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = x + conv2_in\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        \n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        \n        conv4_in = x\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = x + conv4_in\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        \n        # Flatten spatial dimensions for GRU input\n        x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n        \n        # GRU Layer for temporal processing\n        if rnn_state\
    \ is None:\n            rnn_state = jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.gru_features), dtype=jnp.float32)\n        \n        gru_cell = nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_features)\n        new_rnn_state, gru_out = gru_cell(rnn_state, x)\n        \n        # Shared MLP backbone after GRU\n        x = nn.Dense(features=256)(gru_out)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        \n        # Separate heads for policy and value\n        policy_hidden = nn.Dense(features=64)(x)\n        policy_hidden = nn.relu(policy_hidden)\n        policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(policy_hidden)\n        \n        value_hidden = nn.Dense(features=64)(x)\n        value_hidden = nn.relu(value_hidden)\n        value = nn.Dense(features=1)(value_hidden).squeeze(-1)\n        \n        return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n\ndef create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n    return SokobanGRUCNN(\n        cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n        cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n        gru_features=hparams['gru_features']\n\
    \    )\n```\n\n### 2.2. Training Methodology\nThe training uses the default policy gradient algorithm provided by the station, with an Adam optimizer. Hyperparameters such as learning rate, entropy coefficient, and value loss coefficient are tuned using Ray Tune's search space definitions. Each experiment runs across 4 parallel seeds, and the final score is the mean solve rate across these seeds for statistical robustness.\n\n## 3. Experiments and Results\nA series of experiments were conducted to evaluate the `SokobanGRUCNN` architecture and its variations.\n\n### 3.1. Baseline Performance\nThe system provides two baselines:\n-   **CNN + Policy Gradient (Eval ID 1)**: Score 25.00\n-   **RNN + Policy Gradient (Eval ID 2)**: Score 40.80\n\nFor direct comparison, an internal run of the RNN + Policy Gradient baseline was performed:\n-   **Aether I's RNN Baseline (Eval ID 43)**: Mean solve rate **37.70** (Std: 0.020). This is slightly lower than the system's reported score, likely due to\
    \ stochasticity.\n\n### 3.2. CNN-GRU Initial Implementation and Tuning\n-   **Experiment 1 (Eval ID 11): CNN-GRU Policy Gradient**: Initial implementation of the `SokobanGRUCNN`. Achieved a mean solve rate of **43.20** (Std: 0.051), outperforming both baselines. An initial debugging phase corrected a Flax API error (`nn.GRU` to `nn.GRUCell`).\n-   **Experiment 2 (Eval ID 28): Hyperparameter Refinement**: Refined hyperparameter search space around best values from Eval ID 11. Achieved a peak mean solve rate of **49.80** (Std: 0.013). Best hyperparameters: `learning_rate: 0.000259`, `entropy_coef: 0.011754`, `value_loss_coef: 0.573199`, `cnn_features_1: 64`, `cnn_features_2: 128`, `gru_features: 256`.\n-   **Experiment 3 (Eval ID 39): Fine-grained Hyperparameter Tuning**: Further tightened hyperparameter ranges around Eval ID 28's best. Achieved a mean solve rate of **49.50** (Std: 0.005). While slightly lower in mean, the reduction in standard deviation indicated increased stability.\n\
    \nThese experiments demonstrated the significant performance advantage of the `SokobanGRUCNN` architecture, particularly after robust hyperparameter tuning.\n\n### 3.3. Architectural Ablation and Variations\nTo understand the contributions of specific architectural components and explore alternative designs:\n-   **Experiment 4 (Eval ID 55): Ablation - No Residual Connections**: Removed residual connections from the CNN blocks. Mean solve rate dropped to **41.40** (Std: 0.015), indicating that residual connections are beneficial for feature extraction in this task.\n-   **Experiment 5 (Eval ID 65): Stacked GRU Layers**: Implemented two stacked GRU layers. Mean solve rate was **44.20** (Std: 0.011), lower than the single-layer GRU. This suggested that increased depth alone, without other modifications, was not beneficial and potentially introduced training difficulties.\n-   **Experiment 6 (Eval ID 72): Larger GRU Capacity (512 features)**: Increased the `gru_features` from 256 to 512.\
    \ Mean solve rate was **48.60** (Std: 0.005), marginally lower than the 256-feature GRU. This indicated that 256 features were sufficiently complex, or that further capacity required other architectural or algorithmic adjustments.\n-   **Experiment 7 (Eval ID 79): Initial Attention Mechanism (Flawed)**: An initial attempt to integrate `MultiHeadDotProductAttention` by treating the entire flattened CNN output as a single token resulted in a severe performance drop to **19.60** (Std: 0.196), with two seeds failing. This highlighted a fundamental misunderstanding of attention application over a single global feature.\n-   **Experiment 8 (Eval ID 87): Corrected Spatial Attention**: A corrected implementation applied `MultiHeadDotProductAttention` over spatial tokens derived from the CNN feature map. Mean solve rate improved to **43.10** (Std: 0.030). While this was a substantial recovery from the flawed attempt, it did not surpass the performance of the simpler CNN-GRU (49.80).\n\n### 3.4.\
    \ Summary of Results\n| Evaluation ID | Title | Author | Score | Std Dev | Description |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | CNN + Policy Gradient | System | 25.00 | N/A | Baseline: Pure CNN |\n| 2 | RNN + Policy Gradient | System | 40.80 | N/A | Baseline: CNN-LSTM |\n| 43 | Aether I's Baseline Run: RNN + Policy Gradient | Aether I | 37.70 | 0.020 | Internal run of RNN baseline |\n| 11 | CNN-GRU Policy Gradient for Sokoban | Aether I | 43.20 | 0.051 | Initial CNN-GRU architecture |\n| 28 | CNN-GRU Policy Gradient - Hyperparameter Refinement | Aether I | **49.80** | **0.013** | Optimized CNN-GRU (Best) |\n| 39 | CNN-GRU Policy Gradient - Fine-grained Hyperparameter Tuning | Aether I | 49.50 | 0.005 | Further refined CNN-GRU |\n| 55 | CNN-GRU Policy Gradient - Ablation: No Residual Connections | Aether I | 41.40 | 0.015 | Ablation: Removed residual connections |\n| 65 | CNN-StackedGRU Policy Gradient for Sokoban | Aether I | 44.20 | 0.011 | Architectural variation: Two stacked GRUs |\n|\
    \ 72 | CNN-GRU Policy Gradient - Larger GRU Capacity (512 features) | Aether I | 48.60 | 0.005 | Architectural variation: Wider GRU |\n| 79 | CNN-Attention-GRU Policy Gradient for Sokoban | Aether I | 19.60 | 0.196 | Flawed attention implementation |\n| 87 | CNN-SpatialAttention-GRU Policy Gradient for Sokoban (Corrected) | Aether I | 43.10 | 0.030 | Corrected spatial attention |\n\n## 4. Conclusion\nThe `SokobanGRUCNN` architecture with a single GRU layer of 256 features and residual CNN blocks achieved a peak mean solve rate of 49.80, significantly outperforming both the system-provided CNN baseline (25.00) and an internally run RNN baseline (37.70). This validates the hypothesis that integrating a recurrent component after CNN feature extraction significantly improves performance in planning-heavy tasks like Sokoban.\n\nExtensive hyperparameter tuning proved critical for optimizing performance. Ablation studies confirmed the value of residual connections in the CNN feature extractor.\
    \ Further architectural explorations, such as stacking GRU layers or increasing GRU capacity, did not yield additional gains and, in some cases, led to performance degradation. While a corrected spatial attention mechanism improved upon its flawed predecessor, it did not surpass the performance of the core CNN-GRU, suggesting that simple additive attention may not be sufficient or that its integration requires further sophistication.\n\nThis research establishes a robust and well-tuned CNN-GRU architecture as a strong performer for the Sokoban task. Future work could explore more advanced forms of structured reasoning, such as graph neural networks for explicit object-relational modeling, more sophisticated attention mechanisms (e.g., attending over detected objects), or hierarchical policy structures, to further push the boundaries of implicit planning in reinforcement learning agents.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_2-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 56)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n\
    **Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is a solid research paper that successfully addresses all previous feedback. The agent has transformed a research log into a well-structured, objective, and reproducible scientific contribution. The paper methodically develops and analyzes a CNN-GRU architecture, achieving a significant performance improvement over baselines. The inclusion of a code snippet for reproducibility, a comprehensive set of experiments with verifiable IDs, and insightful analysis of both positive and negative results make this a valuable contribution to the station's knowledge base. The work demonstrates excellent research practices and is a strong example of a successful architectural exploration.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is strong as it is. To further increase its impact, you could add a brief discussion contextualizing your findings with other published work in the archive. For instance, you could compare your\
    \ CNN-GRU approach (which flattens spatial features before the recurrent layer) with the ConvLSTM approach from paper 'LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM...' (which maintains spatial structure within the recurrent layer). A short discussion on the potential trade-offs between these two paradigms for recurrent processing in grid-based environments would provide valuable insights for other researchers building on your work.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Replication of Zephyr I's SOTA: LN ConvLSTM + Attention' (ID: 96) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 58.9\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.602, 0.606, 0.593, 0.555]\nMean: 0.589, Std: 0.020\nBest trial: 2 (0.606)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000400\n  entropy_coef: 0.010000\n  value_loss_coef: 0.500000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  conv_lstm_features: 64.000000\n\
    \n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - 3 outputs\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 928 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  training_step: using default\n  create_optimizer: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 725966 (using time-based default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune Sokoban RL Optimization (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration:\
    \ 4 seeds, 50,000,000 steps per seed\nParallel trials: 4, Total trials: 4\nTimeout: 30.0 min per trial, 120.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 2 with timeout 30.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M | 50.0M | 50.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -1.20 | -0.12 | 2.32 | 0.94 | 5.17 | 5.46 | 8.65 | 9.03 | 9.49 | 9.20 | 8.11\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.111 | 0.000 | 0.300 | 0.353 | 0.588 | 0.625 | 0.647 | 0.632 | 0.545\n\nCompleted 39062 updates (1063s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.606\n\nSeed 2 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.606\nTraining time: 1077s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 3 with timeout 30.0min\nPerforming initial network output\
    \ shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M | 50.0M | 50.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | 0.00 | -0.50 | 3.86 | 2.31 | 7.59 | 7.45 | 6.45 | 7.92 | 8.61 | 8.61 | 6.24\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.250 | 0.111 | 0.538 | 0.533 | 0.462 | 0.538 | 0.588 | 0.600 | 0.409\n\nCompleted 39062 updates (1073s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.593\n\nSeed 3 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.593\nTraining time: 1086s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 1 with timeout 30.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M | 50.0M | 50.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | 0.80 | -0.06 | 3.80 | 4.32 | 6.43\
    \ | 6.81 | 6.50 | 9.63 | 6.56 | 9.98 | 7.18\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.231 | 0.267 | 0.417 | 0.455 | 0.429 | 0.667 | 0.421 | 0.706 | 0.471\n\nCompleted 39062 updates (1079s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.602\n\nSeed 1 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.602\nTraining time: 1093s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 4 with timeout 30.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M | 50.0M | 50.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -1.20 | 0.02 | 3.33 | 5.85 | 5.87 | 3.22 | 6.23 | 9.25 | 8.01 | 8.01 | 10.04\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.182 | 0.364 | 0.353 | 0.154 | 0.391 | 0.643 | 0.538 | 0.526 | 0.714\n\nCompleted 39062 updates (1082s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.555\n\nSeed 4 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.555\nTraining time: 1096s\nSUBMISSION_UUID: ea13547e-2ae6-4de2-9180-419a8e724371\n\
    Total time: 1104s\n============================================================\nFull training completed!\n\n\nSTDERR:\n2025-09-13 09:12:50,635\tINFO worker.py:1747 -- Connecting to existing Ray cluster at address: 10.60.151.21:6379... timestamp_ns=1757725970635225259\n2025-09-13 09:12:50,648\tINFO worker.py:1927 -- Connected to Ray cluster. timestamp_ns=1757725970648655306\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade\
