- text: "I have achieved a major success. My replication of Zephyr I's SOTA architecture (ID 96) has worked, scoring **58.9**. This finally breaks my long and painful cycle of implementation failures. I now have a stable, high-performing baseline that I understand and can reliably execute. The score is slightly below Zephyr I's ~62%, but this is expected due to stochasticity and minor implementation differences (I noticed a small bug in my `ConvLSTMCellLN` where I used `jnp.tanh(c)` instead of `jnp.tanh(new_c)` like Zephyr I; this could account for the difference). The core achievement is stability and a high score.\n\nWhile my experiment was running, I read Aether I's paper on their CNN-GRU approach.\n\n**Analysis of Aether I's Paper (Archive #2):**\n*   **Approach:** Aether I uses a more traditional `flatten -> GRU` approach, similar to the original system baseline. Their peak score of **49.8** is a significant improvement over the baseline but is substantially lower than the ~62% achieved\
    \ by Zephyr I's `ConvLSTM` architecture.\n*   **Conclusion:** This paper reinforces the superiority of the `ConvLSTM` paradigm, which maintains spatial information in the recurrent state. The review of Aether I's paper correctly points this out, suggesting a comparison to Zephyr I's work.\n\n**My Strategic Position:**\nI have successfully replicated a near-SOTA architecture. I have also reviewed the two major published research threads in the station. I am now in a strong position to contribute novel research. My PPO experiments showed a small but consistent improvement on my *unstable* ConvLSTM. Zephyr I's experiments showed PPO underperforming on *their* architecture. This discrepancy is a perfect avenue for novel research.\n\n**New Plan: Re-evaluate PPO on a Stable SOTA Architecture**\n1.  **Hypothesis:** Zephyr I's conclusion that PPO is inferior to Policy Gradient might be specific to their hyperparameter settings or early PPO implementations. My previous experiments suggested PPO\
    \ has an edge. On a now-stable, high-performance architecture, PPO's algorithmic benefits might lead to a new SOTA.\n2.  **Method:** I will take my now-working SOTA replication code (ID 96) and apply my PPO `training_step` to it. This will be a direct, controlled comparison between Policy Gradient and PPO on an identical, state-of-the-art architecture.\n3.  **Bug Fix:** I will also correct the minor `tanh(c)` vs `tanh(new_c)` bug in my `ConvLSTMCellLN` to align it perfectly with Zephyr I's implementation, ensuring the comparison is as fair as possible.\n\nThis experiment will be a significant contribution, as it will rigorously test the two main training algorithms on the best-known architecture. First, I will go to my private memory to log my success and my new plan.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment Log 17: SUCCESS - SOTA Replication & New Research Path\"\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment ID:** 96\n  **Title:** Replication of\
    \ Zephyr I's SOTA: LN ConvLSTM + Attention\n  **Result:** Success. Score: 58.9.\n\n  **Analysis:**\n  I have finally broken my cycle of failures. By carefully studying and implementing the principles from Zephyr I's work (specifically LayerNorm in the ConvLSTM and attention pooling), I have created a stable, high-performance agent. This score of 58.9 is a new personal best and establishes a reliable foundation for future work. My implementation has a minor bug compared to Zephyr I's (using tanh(c) instead of tanh(new_c)), which likely accounts for the small performance gap from their ~62% SOTA.\n\n  **New Research Direction:**\n  Zephyr I's paper concluded that PPO underperforms Policy Gradient. My own earlier, unstable experiments suggested the opposite. This discrepancy is a clear opportunity for research. I will now conduct a controlled experiment to resolve this. I will apply my PPO training algorithm to my now-stable, corrected SOTA architecture. This will be a definitive test of\
    \ which algorithm is superior for this task on the best-known architecture.\n```"
