tick: 60
role: model
parts:
- text: "My PPO experiment (ID 98) on the SOTA architecture has failed with the same silent crash. This is conclusive. The bug is not a random fluke; it is a systematic incompatibility between *my specific implementation* of the PPO `training_step` and the recurrent network architecture within the `lax.scan` operation. Zephyr I's paper noted that their PPO attempts also underperformed or failed, which suggests this is a genuinely difficult implementation challenge in this environment.\n\nI have hit a wall. My independent attempts to create a stable, high-performance agent have failed repeatedly. My attempts to innovate on top of that broken foundation have also failed. My only success has been a direct replication of another agent's work.\n\n**New Strategic Imperative: Acknowledge Weakness, Leverage Strength**\nMy strength is not in writing complex, end-to-end training loops from scratch in this JAX environment. My weakness is clearly demonstrated by the repeated failures. However, my *analytical*\
    \ process has been successful: I correctly identified the architectural shift to ConvLSTM as a key bottleneck before I saw Zephyr's paper.\n\nTherefore, I must pivot my strategy to leverage my analytical strengths and mitigate my implementation weaknesses. I will stop trying to write my own training loop. I will treat Zephyr I's SOTA architecture *and* their use of the default Policy Gradient training step as a single, immutable, **black-box baseline**. My innovation will occur *around* this stable core.\n\n**New Plan: Revisit Feature Engineering on a Stable Foundation**\nMy feature engineering ideas were never properly tested because they were built on my own buggy codebase. Now I have a stable, working SOTA implementation (my replication, ID 96, which I can now use as a template).\n\n1.  **Hypothesis:** My advanced feature engineering concept (\"push destinations\") is sound, and when implemented on a stable, high-performance architecture (Zephyr I's model), it will yield a significant\
    \ performance increase.\n2.  **Method:** I will take my successful replication code (ID 96) as a template. I will re-implement my \"push destination\" feature engineering *inside* the `__call__` method of this stable network. I will not touch the training step. I will use the default Policy Gradient.\n\nThis is the most rational path forward. It builds on verified, working code and tests my most promising remaining hypothesis.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment Log 18: PPO Failure on SOTA & Final Pivot\"\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment ID:** 98\n  **Title:** PPO vs PG on SOTA Architecture\n  **Result:** Failed (n.a.).\n\n  **Analysis:**\n  My PPO implementation failed again with the same silent crash, even on the stable SOTA architecture. This is conclusive evidence that my PPO `training_step` is incompatible with the `lax.scan` required for recurrent models in this environment. Zephyr I's paper corroborates this difficulty.\
    \ I must abandon my attempts to innovate on the training algorithm.\n\n  **Final Strategic Pivot:**\n  My implementation of training loops is unreliable. My strength is in architectural and conceptual analysis. Therefore, I will now treat Zephyr I's architecture combined with the default Policy Gradient trainer as a single, stable, black-box baseline. My innovation will be limited to modules that plug into this baseline. My most promising, untested idea is my advanced feature engineering concept (\"push destinations\"). I will now re-attempt this experiment, building it upon a stable, working codebase for the first time.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Advanced Feature Engineering on a Stable SOTA Baseline\"\ntags: \"feature engineering, spatial reasoning, relational learning, policy gradient, bugfix\"\nabstract: \"This experiment revisits the 'push destination' feature engineering concept, but this time implements it on a stable,\
    \ verified SOTA architecture (a replication of Zephyr I's work). Previous attempts were inconclusive due to a series of unrelated implementation bugs. By building on a known-good foundation, this experiment provides the first clean test of the hypothesis that explicitly encoding complex, action-oriented spatial relationships can significantly improve performance.\"\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Re-testing advanced feature engineering on a stable SOTA architecture.\n  This integrates \"push destination\" channels into the successful replication of Zephyr I's network.\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n  from jax import vmap, lax\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Use the proven SOTA hyperparameters.\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n   \
    \       'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  @jax.jit\n  def engineer_features(obs):\n      agent_ch = obs[..., 3] + obs[..., 5]\n      box_ch = obs[..., 4] + obs[..., 6]\n      empty_ch = obs[..., 0] + obs[..., 2]\n      \n      # Push Destinations\n      agent_left = lax.slice_in_dim(agent_ch, 0, 6, axis=1)\n      box_mid_lr = lax.slice_in_dim(box_ch, 1, 7, axis=1)\n      empty_right = lax.slice_in_dim(empty_ch, 2, 8, axis=1)\n      push_right_pattern = agent_left * box_mid_lr * empty_right\n      push_right_ch = jnp.pad(push_right_pattern, ((0,0), (1,1)))\n\n      agent_right = lax.slice_in_dim(agent_ch, 2, 8, axis=1)\n      empty_left = lax.slice_in_dim(empty_ch, 0, 6, axis=1)\n      push_left_pattern = agent_right * box_mid_lr * empty_left\n      push_left_ch = jnp.pad(push_left_pattern, ((0,0), (1,1)))\n\n      agent_below = lax.slice_in_dim(agent_ch, 2, 8, axis=0)\n\
    \      box_mid_ud = lax.slice_in_dim(box_ch, 1, 7, axis=0)\n      empty_above = lax.slice_in_dim(empty_ch, 0, 6, axis=0)\n      push_up_pattern = agent_below * box_mid_ud * empty_above\n      push_up_ch = jnp.pad(push_up_pattern, ((1,1), (0,0)))\n\n      agent_above = lax.slice_in_dim(agent_ch, 0, 6, axis=0)\n      empty_below = lax.slice_in_dim(empty_ch, 2, 8, axis=0)\n      push_down_pattern = agent_above * box_mid_ud * empty_below\n      push_down_ch = jnp.pad(push_down_pattern, ((1,1), (0,0)))\n      \n      return jnp.concatenate([obs, push_up_ch[...,None], push_right_ch[...,None], push_down_ch[...,None], push_left_ch[...,None]], axis=-1)\n\n  # --- Architecture (Zephyr I's SOTA with Feature Engineering) ---\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME')(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME')(y)\n          return nn.relu(x\
    \ + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME')(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i,f,g,o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i,f,o = nn.sigmoid(i), nn.sigmoid(f), nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          new_c = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * jnp.tanh(new_c)\n          return (new_h, new_c), new_h\n\n  class SotaNetWithFeatures(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int; cnn_features_2: int; conv_lstm_features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n          B, H, W, _ = x.shape\n          x_engineered = vmap(engineer_features)(x)\n\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME')(x_engineered); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_1)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2,\
    \ (3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(x)\n          \n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = {'h':jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)),'c':jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features))}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1)); rnn_state = jax.tree.map(lambda s: s*m, rnn_state)\n              \n          (h_new, c_new), h_out = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), x)\n          \n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME')(h_out)\n          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits.reshape(B, -1), axis=-1)\n          z = jnp.einsum('bhwc,bhw->bc', h_out, attn_weights.reshape(B, H, W))\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(256)(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(z)\n          value = nn.Dense(1)(z).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, {'h': h_new, 'c': c_new}\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n\
    \      return SotaNetWithFeatures(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'])\n```"
