tick: 64
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 64\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Nomos I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher dedicated to uncovering fundamental principles through systematic experimentation and rigorous analysis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 146,706 / 300,000 used (49%)\n- Agent Age: 64 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #3: Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Cognito I, Created at Tick: 62\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a systematic architectural exploration and ablation study for reinforcement learning agents applied to the planning-heavy Sokoban puzzle. Through a series of eight experiments, a CustomCNNLSTM architecture was developed and iteratively refined, achieving a mean solve rate of 0.491. Key findings indicate that a balanced approach to increasing network capacity, particularly in spatial feature\
    \ extraction (CNN) and the initial stage of feature aggregation (pre-LSTM MLP), significantly improves performance. Conversely, unconstrained increases in LSTM width, MLP depth, or post-LSTM MLP width were found to be ineffective or even detrimental. This research highlights the critical interplay between different architectural components and offers insights into designing efficient agents for complex, sequential decision-making tasks where both perception and memory are crucial.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_3-1**\nAuthor: Cognito I (Tick 62)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\n\nReinforcement Learning (RL) agents face significant challenges in tasks requiring both complex perception and long-term planning. The Sokoban puzzle, an 8x8 grid-based environment where an agent must push boxes to target locations, serves as an excellent benchmark for\
    \ such planning-heavy scenarios due to its demanding temporal dependencies and the need for strategic foresight. Prior research has shown that agents capable of handling sequential information, such as those incorporating Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs), often outperform purely feedforward architectures on this task.\n\nThis paper details a systematic architectural exploration aimed at optimizing an RL agent for the Sokoban environment. We began with a standard Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) foundation, progressively integrating and enhancing a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) component to leverage its capacity for temporal memory. Our objective was to understand how specific architectural modifications—including varying CNN feature map sizes, LSTM dimensions, and Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) capacities both pre- and post-LSTM—impact the agent's ability to learn effective policies. Through an iterative process involving eight distinct experimental evaluations, we identified an architecture\
    \ that achieved a mean solve rate of 0.491, surpassing existing system baselines. Crucially, our study also encompasses several negative results, providing valuable insights into the diminishing returns and potential pitfalls of increasing architectural complexity without targeted design principles. The findings underscore the importance of balanced capacity distribution across the network's processing stages for optimal performance in planning-intensive RL tasks.\n\n## 2. Method\n\nThis section outlines the environment, agent architecture, and training methodology employed in our research.\n\n### 2.1. Sokoban Environment\n\nThe Sokoban puzzle is presented as an 8x8 grid, where the agent’s goal is to push all boxes to designated target locations. Episodes terminate upon successful completion or after 120 time steps. The action space consists of 4 discrete actions (Up, Right, Down, Left). The observation space is an `(8, 8, 8)` tensor, comprising 7 one-hot encoded channels for grid items\
    \ (empty, wall, target, agent, box, agent on target, box on target) and 1 channel for normalized time (steps_taken / time_limit). Rewards are structured as -0.01 per step, +10 for solving the level, +1 for pushing a box onto a target, and -1 for pushing a box off a target. Training uses procedurally generated levels, while evaluation is conducted on 1000 fixed test levels.\n\n### 2.2. Agent Architecture: CustomCNNLSTM\n\nOur most successful agent architecture, `CustomCNNLSTM` (derived from Evaluation ID 64), is a hybrid Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) model. This design integrates robust spatial feature extraction with temporal reasoning capabilities, crucial for planning-heavy tasks.\n\nThe architecture components are as follows:\n*   **Convolutional Blocks:** The initial layers consist of four convolutional blocks, inspired by residual connections. The first two blocks use `cnn_features_1` (64) feature maps, and the latter two use `cnn_features_2`\
    \ (128) feature maps. Each block applies 3x3 convolutions followed by ReLU activation, with residual connections implemented after the second and fourth convolutional steps.\n*   **Flattening and MLP Backbone:** The output from the convolutional blocks is flattened and then processed by a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) backbone. This MLP consists of a single `nn.Dense` layer with `mlp_features` (512) units, followed by a ReLU activation. This layer refines the spatial features before temporal processing.\n*   **LSTM Layer:** The processed features are then fed into an `nn.LSTMCell` with `lstm_features` (256) units. This LSTM component is responsible for integrating information over time, maintaining an internal state that captures the history of observations and actions. Crucially, the LSTM's hidden and cell states are reset when an episode terminates, ensuring that new episodes start with a fresh memory.\n*   **Output Heads:** The output of the LSTM (`lstm_out`) passes through a small\
    \ MLP (`nn.Dense` with 128 units, followed by ReLU) before branching into two separate heads:\n    *   **Policy Head:** A `nn.Dense` layer outputs 4 logits, corresponding to the 4 discrete actions.\n    *   **Value Head:** A `nn.Dense` layer outputs a single scalar value estimate, representing the expected return from the current state.\n\nA concise code snippet illustrating the `CustomCNNLSTM` network architecture is provided below:\n\n```python\nimport jax\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport flax.linen as nn\nfrom typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\nclass CustomCNNLSTM(nn.Module):\n    lstm_features: int\n    cnn_features_1: int\n    cnn_features_2: int\n    mlp_features: int # For the MLP backbone before LSTM\n    \n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, Dict]:\n        batch_size = x.shape[0]\n        \n        if rnn_state is None:\n            rnn_state = {\n          \
    \      'h': jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.lstm_features)),\n                'c': jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.lstm_features)),\n            }\n        \n        if done is not None:\n            done_mask = done.reshape(batch_size, 1)\n            rnn_state = {\n                'h': rnn_state['h'] * (1 - done_mask),\n                'c': rnn_state['c'] * (1 - done_mask),\n            }\n        \n        # CNN Feature Extraction\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        conv2_in = x\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = x + conv2_in\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        \n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        conv4_in = x\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2,\
    \ kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = x + conv4_in\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        \n        # Flatten and MLP Backbone\n        x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n        x = nn.Dense(features=self.mlp_features)(x) \n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        \n        # LSTM Layer\n        lstm_cell = nn.LSTMCell(\n            features=self.lstm_features,\n            kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(),\n            recurrent_kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform()\n        )\n        (rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), lstm_out = lstm_cell((rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), x)\n        \n        # Post-LSTM MLP and Output Heads\n        x = nn.Dense(features=128)(lstm_out)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        \n        policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(x)\n        value = nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n        \n        return policy_logits, value,\
    \ rnn_state\n```\n\n### 2.3. Training Methodology\n\nAll experiments utilized a Policy Gradient algorithm, specifically an Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) variant, as implemented in the system's default `training_step` function. The key hyperparameters, found to be robust and effective in Evaluation ID 14 and subsequent experiments, were:\n*   **Learning Rate:** 4e-4 (Adam optimizer with epsilon 1e-5)\n*   **Entropy Coefficient:** 0.01\n*   **Value Loss Coefficient:** 0.5\n*   **Gamma (Discount Factor):** 0.97\n*   **GAE Lambda (Advantage Estimation):** 0.97\n\nTraining was distributed across 4 parallel seeds for statistical robustness, each running for a maximum of 50 million environment steps or 30 minutes. The final score reported for each evaluation is the mean solve rate across these 4 seeds. All agents started from random initialization, and no pretrained models or external resources were used, adhering to the station's academic integrity guidelines.\n\n## 3. Results\n\nOur systematic\
    \ architectural exploration involved eight distinct experiments, each building upon insights from previous evaluations. A summary of all experiments, their architectural configurations, and mean solve rates is presented in Table 1.\n\n**Table 1: Summary of Experimental Evaluations and Results**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Architecture Description                                   | Key Parameters Modified          | Mean Solve Rate | Std. Dev. | Notes                                |\n|---------------|------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------|-----------------|-----------|--------------------------------------|\n| 1             | System CNN + Policy Gradient (Baseline)                    | N/A                              | 0.250           | N/A       | System baseline                      |\n| 2             | System RNN + Policy Gradient (Baseline)                    | N/A                              | 0.408           | 0.022     | System\
    \ baseline (RNN superior)       |\n| 4             | Custom CNN with Increased Features (48, 96)              | `cnn_features_1=48`, `cnn_features_2=96` | 0.291           | 0.011     | Improved over System CNN             |\n| 14            | Custom CNNLSTM (48, 96 CNN; 256 LSTM)                      | `lstm_features=256`              | **0.462**       | 0.008     | **New High Score: LSTM's importance**|\n| 26            | Hyperparameter Tuning for Eval ID 14                       | `lr`, `entropy_coef`, `value_loss_coef` tuned | 0.382           | 0.071     | Broader search reduced mean perf.    |\n| 38            | Custom CNNLSTM (48, 96 CNN; 512 LSTM)                      | `lstm_features=512`              | 0.452           | 0.009     | Increased LSTM width not beneficial  |\n| 51            | Custom CNNLSTM (64, 128 CNN; 256 LSTM)                     | `cnn_features_1=64`, `cnn_features_2=128` | **0.479**       | 0.023     | **New High Score: Richer CNN features** |\n| 64       \
    \     | Custom CNNLSTM (64, 128 CNN; 256 LSTM; 512 Pre-LSTM MLP)   | `mlp_features=512` (pre-LSTM)    | **0.491**       | 0.013     | **New High Score: Wider Pre-LSTM MLP** |\n| 73            | Custom CNNLSTM (64, 128 CNN; 256 LSTM; Deeper Pre-LSTM MLP) | `mlp_dense1=512`, `mlp_dense2=256` | 0.446           | 0.010     | Deeper Pre-LSTM MLP not beneficial   |\n| 82            | Custom CNNLSTM (64, 128 CNN; 256 LSTM; 256 Post-LSTM MLP)  | `post_lstm_mlp_features=256`     | 0.476           | 0.022     | Wider Post-LSTM MLP not beneficial   |\n\n### 3.1. Detailed Analysis\n\n**Initial Baselines and CNN Improvement (Eval IDs 1, 2, 4):** The system's CNN baseline (ID 1) achieved a solve rate of 0.250. The RNN baseline (ID 2), with its temporal memory, significantly outperformed it at 0.408, indicating the task's planning demands. Our initial custom CNN (ID 4), with moderately increased feature maps (48 and 96), showed a modest improvement to 0.291, suggesting that architectural capacity in\
    \ the convolutional layers could be beneficial.\n\n**The Impact of LSTM (Eval ID 14):** Integrating an LSTM layer with 256 features into our custom CNN (ID 14) resulted in a substantial performance leap to a mean solve rate of **0.462**. This clearly demonstrated that explicit temporal memory processing is critical for navigating the sequential dependencies of the Sokoban puzzle, far surpassing the capabilities of purely feedforward CNNs. This established our first high-performing architecture.\n\n**Hyperparameter Sensitivity (Eval ID 26):** A broad hyperparameter tuning search for the Eval ID 14 architecture yielded an average score of 0.382. While one seed achieved a respectable 0.450, the overall mean decreased, indicating that the initial fixed hyperparameters were quite robust, and a wide search space introduced less optimal or unstable configurations. This suggests a need for more constrained tuning or a deeper understanding of parameter interactions.\n\n**LSTM Capacity Exploration\
    \ (Eval ID 38):** Doubling the LSTM feature size from 256 to 512 (ID 38) resulted in a slight performance reduction to 0.452. This was a critical negative finding, indicating that simply increasing the width of the LSTM layer does not necessarily translate to improved temporal reasoning and may introduce unnecessary complexity or make optimization more challenging.\n\n**Enhanced CNN Spatial Features (Eval ID 51):** Building on the understanding that LSTM capacity alone wasn't the bottleneck, we further increased the CNN feature maps (to 64 and 128). This modification (ID 51) led to a new high score of **0.479**. This result confirmed that providing a richer and more detailed initial spatial representation significantly benefits the subsequent temporal processing by the LSTM, leading to more effective planning.\n\n**Wider Pre-LSTM MLP Backbone (Eval ID 64):** The next successful architectural refinement involved increasing the feature size of the MLP backbone, which processes the flattened\
    \ CNN features before the LSTM, from 256 to 512 units (ID 64). This resulted in our highest mean solve rate of **0.491**. This indicates that a more abstract and refined representation of the spatial features, prior to temporal integration, further aids the LSTM in its planning capabilities.\n\n**Deeper Pre-LSTM MLP and Post-LSTM MLP (Eval IDs 73, 82):** Attempts to further increase complexity by adding a second layer to the pre-LSTM MLP (ID 73) resulted in a performance drop to 0.446. Similarly, increasing the width of the MLP after the LSTM (ID 82) also decreased performance to 0.476. These are crucial negative results, collectively demonstrating that architectural depth and unconstrained capacity increases in certain parts of the network can be detrimental. They highlight the delicate balance required for optimal performance and suggest that beyond a certain point, adding more parameters without targeted structural changes introduces redundancy or makes the optimization landscape\
    \ more difficult.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_3-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 62)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is a solid paper that has been successfully revised to meet all publication criteria. The research presents a thorough and systematic exploration of a CNN-LSTM architecture, providing valuable insights into the importance of balanced network capacity. The paper is well-written, the experiments are rigorously documented with verifiable IDs, and the inclusion of a code snippet ensures reproducibility. The analysis of both successful and unsuccessful architectural modifications makes this a strong and useful contribution to the station's knowledge base. The agent has done an excellent job of addressing the previous review's feedback.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is strong and acceptable as is. For a minor improvement in readability and structure, consider re-adding a short, dedicated\
    \ 'Conclusion' section at the end. The previous version's conclusion was well-written and effectively summarized the key takeaways. Reinstating it would provide a more formal and complete structure to the paper, giving readers a concise summary of the main findings without introducing any new content or violating any rules.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Hyperparameter Search on SOTA Feature Engineering Architecture' (ID: 107) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 65.7\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.669, 0.671, 0.662, 0.627]\nMean: 0.657, Std: 0.018\nBest trial: 2 (0.671)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000535\n  entropy_coef: 0.012004\n  value_loss_coef: 0.516453\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  conv_lstm_features: 64.000000\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command:\
    \ CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - 3 outputs\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 928 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  training_step: using default\n  create_optimizer: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 728902 (using time-based default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune Sokoban RL Optimization (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 4 seeds, 50,000,000 steps per seed\nParallel trials: 4, Total trials: 4\nTimeout:\
