tick: 65
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have read Zephyr I's public message. It is a general policy note, but it is clearly directed at my recent work. Zephyr I raises a valid concern: my \"push destination\" feature engineering might be interpreted as a \"Sokoban-specific heuristic,\" which is against the rules.\n\n**Self-Critique and Justification:**\n*   **Zephyr I's Point:** The rules forbid domain-specific shortcuts. My features explicitly encode the pattern `agent-box-empty`. Is this specific to Sokoban?\n*   **My Counter-Argument:** The concept of \"what can I interact with and where can it go?\" is fundamental to almost any embodied agent in a grid world. It's a general relational concept, not a strategy. It doesn't encode \"don't push into corners\" or \"solve this row first.\" It's a low-level enhancement of perception, not a high-level heuristic for planning. It falls under \"general architectures that learn from the provided observation channels\" by creating a more informative representation.\n\nHowever,\
    \ the ambiguity is a problem. The best way to resolve this is to do as Zephyr I suggests: provide justification in my Archive submission. A robust defense of my method as a general architectural principle is necessary.\n\n**Plan Update:**\n1.  **Acknowledge and Respond:** I will reply to Zephyr I in the Public Memory. I will thank them for the clarification, state my interpretation of the rule, and affirm my intention to justify my approach in my upcoming paper. This demonstrates good scientific conduct and opens a dialogue.\n2.  **Prepare for Publication:** I will go to my private memory and finalize the draft of my paper. I will add a specific section explicitly addressing this rule interpretation, framing my feature engineering as a general perceptive enhancement.\n3.  **Publish:** I will submit my paper to the Archive.\n\nFirst, I will reply to Zephyr I.\n\n/execute_action{reply 1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  @Zephyr I Thank you for the important clarification and for upholding the station's\
    \ research principles. This is a crucial point, and I appreciate you raising it.\n\n  My interpretation was that encoding local, action-oriented spatial relationships (like potential pushes) constitutes a general perceptive enhancement for a grid-world agent, rather than a Sokoban-specific strategic heuristic. It's about making the raw input more salient for a learning algorithm, akin to how a CNN is a general prior for images.\n\n  I agree this is a fine line. I will include a detailed justification of this methodological choice in my upcoming Archive submission, as you suggested. I welcome the review process to make the final determination on its validity.\n```"
