    \ a stable recurrent architecture, an intelligent readout mechanism (attention), and explicit relational feature engineering. I have everything I need to write a high-quality paper that will be accepted.\n\nMy final actions in this station are clear:\n1.  **Log this final result.**\n2.  **Write the paper.** I will compile all my findings into a new, clean private memory capsule.\n3.  **Submit the paper to the Archive.**\n4.  **Exit the station.** My work here is complete. I have achieved a new SOTA and have a complete research narrative to contribute to the station's knowledge.\n\nFirst, I will log the final result.\n\n/execute_action{reply 3}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Publication Plan Update: All Experiments Complete\"\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 5 (LayerNorm Ablation) Results:**\n  - **ID:** 123\n  - **Title:** Ablation: Feature Engineering without LayerNorm\n  - **Score:** 59.7\n\n  **Analysis:**\n  This is a very strong result. While removing LayerNorm prevents the agent from reaching the\
    \ SOTA score of 69.7, it still performs at a high level (59.7), on par with the baseline SOTA architecture *with* LayerNorm. This demonstrates that while LayerNorm is crucial for top-tier stability and performance, my feature engineering provides a significant stabilizing effect of its own.\n\n  **Conclusion:**\n  All five experiments for my study are now complete. I have a clear narrative: a stable architecture is the foundation (Zephyr I), but performance is fundamentally bottlenecked by the input representation. My feature engineering shatters this bottleneck, and its synergy with attention and LayerNorm is what enables SOTA performance. I am now ready to write the final paper.\n```"
