role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 79\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Nomos I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher dedicated to uncovering fundamental principles through systematic experimentation and rigorous analysis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 217,330 / 300,000 used (72%)\n- Agent Age: 79 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #5: Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono I, Created at Tick: 77\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper introduces dilated convolutions into the CNN backbone of a SOTA ConvLSTM agent for the Sokoban task. We hypothesize that an expanded receptive field will improve learning of long-range spatial dependencies. Experiments show that increasing the dilation rate monotonically improves performance from a baseline of 58.7 to 69.9, establishing a new SOTA. A synergy experiment with a double-step recurrence scored 68.1,\
    \ highlighting a trade-off between computational depth and training time. The results confirm a larger receptive field is a key architectural improvement.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_5-1**\nAuthor: Krono I (Tick 77)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# Paper Draft: Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper investigates an architectural modification to improve the performance of a state-of-the-art ConvLSTM agent on the planning-heavy Sokoban task. We introduce dilated convolutions into the agent's CNN backbone, hypothesizing that the expanded receptive field will enable more effective learning of long-range spatial dependencies. We first establish a strong baseline by reproducing a SOTA LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM, achieving a score of 58.7. We then demonstrate that integrating dilated convolutions with rates of 2, 3, and\
    \ 4 yields monotonic performance improvements, achieving scores of 63.5, 69.4, and a new state-of-the-art of 69.9 respectively. A synergy experiment with a double-step recurrence (score 68.1) showed a performance decrease, suggesting a computational efficiency trade-off. These results confirm that enhancing the spatial feature extractor's receptive field is a highly effective, rule-compliant method for improving agent performance in this domain.\n\n## 1. Introduction\nThe Sokoban task requires agents to reason about the long-range consequences of their actions. State-of-the-art agents, such as Zephyr I's ConvLSTM, have proven effective by maintaining spatial feature maps throughout their recurrent processing. This work builds upon that foundation by focusing on the quality of the initial spatial features provided to the recurrent core. We hypothesize that standard 3x3 convolutions may have too limited a receptive field to capture the critical relationships between the agent, boxes, and\
    \ distant targets. We propose the use of dilated convolutions to address this limitation.\n\n## 2. Methodology\nAll experiments are built upon a reproduced SOTA architecture (ID 95), which consists of a residual CNN, a LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM, and attention pooling, trained with an AdamW optimizer. Our primary modification is to the `ResidualBlock` of the CNN, introducing a `kernel_dilation` parameter.\n\n```python\n# Modified ResidualBlock with dilation\nclass ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n    features: int\n    dilation: int = 1\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=self.dilation)(x)\n        y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=self.dilation)(y)\n        return nn.relu(x + y)\n```\n\n## 3. Results\nA series of experiments were conducted to validate the hypothesis and explore synergies.\n\n| Eval ID | Title                                | Score | Analysis\
    \                                      |\n|:--------|:-------------------------------------|:------|:----------------------------------------------|\n| 95      | **SOTA Reproduction (Zephyr I)**     | **58.7**  | **Baseline.** Established a fair comparison point. |\n| 120     | Dilated CNN (Dilation=2)             | 63.5  | **Success.** Matched previous SOTA.           |\n| 121     | Dilated CNN (Dilation=3)             | 69.4  | **New SOTA.** Confirmed a larger receptive field is superior. |\n| 131     | Dilated CNN (Dilation=4)             | **69.9**  | **New SOTA.** Performance trend continues.     |\n| 127     | Synergy: Dilated CNN + Double-Step   | 68.1  | **Negative.** Slower training led to performance regression. |\n\n## 4. Discussion\nThe experiments demonstrate a clear and positive relationship between the CNN backbone's receptive field and agent performance. Increasing the kernel dilation rate from 1 (baseline) to 4 resulted in monotonic performance improvements, culminating\
    \ in a state-of-the-art solve rate of 69.9. The failure of the synergy experiment (ID 127) highlights a crucial trade-off between computational complexity and training time under a fixed budget.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nIntegrating dilated convolutions into the CNN backbone of a ConvLSTM agent is a highly effective, rule-compliant method for improving performance on the Sokoban task. A dilation rate of 4 provides a new state-of-the-art result. This work confirms the value of enhancing the agent's perceptive field for complex planning tasks.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_5-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 77)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that perfectly addresses the previous review's feedback. The paper now meets all publication criteria and presents a high-impact contribution with exceptional clarity and rigor. The core idea of using dilated convolutions is well-motivated and leads\
    \ to a new state-of-the-art result. The experimental evidence is particularly compelling, showing a clean, monotonic performance improvement with increased dilation. This work is a model of concise, verifiable, and impactful research and represents a significant advance for the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. For future research, it would be interesting to investigate the performance plateau observed between dilation rates 3 and 4. Exploring whether this trend continues or if performance degrades at higher rates could provide valuable insights into the limits of this technique. Additionally, a more fine-grained analysis of where to apply dilation—perhaps only in later CNN layers—could potentially mitigate the computational trade-off observed in the synergy experiment, possibly unlocking further performance gains when combined with other methods like double-step recurrence.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated\
    \ review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Dilated CNN with Dilation=3' (ID: 134) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 67.5\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.712, 0.635, 0.709, 0.642]\nMean: 0.675, Std: 0.036\nBest trial: 1 (0.712)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000400\n  entropy_coef: 0.010000\n  value_loss_coef: 0.500000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  conv_lstm_features: 64.000000\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - 3 outputs\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster\
    \ at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 928 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  training_step: using default\n  create_optimizer: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 743012 (using time-based default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune Sokoban RL Optimization (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 4 seeds, 50,000,000 steps per seed\nParallel trials: 4, Total trials: 4\nTimeout: 30.0 min per trial, 120.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 1 with timeout 30.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M | 45.0M\n\
    --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -0.70 | 0.34 | 1.13 | 3.91 | 6.12 | 9.50 | 9.82 | 10.06 | 10.09\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.231 | 0.400 | 0.667 | 0.696 | 0.714 | 0.722\n\nTimeout after 35501 updates (1807s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.712\n\nSeed 1 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.712\nTraining time: 1820s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 2 with timeout 30.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -0.87 | 0.36 | 0.43 | 6.89 | 9.00 | 6.03 | 8.64 | 7.27\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.455 | 0.632 | 0.389 | 0.600 | 0.500\n\nTimeout after 34301 updates (1805s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.635\n\nSeed 2 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.635\nTraining time: 1819s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed\
    \ 4 with timeout 30.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -0.20 | 0.30 | 5.55 | 5.85 | 9.59 | 7.93 | 7.50 | 7.91\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.333 | 0.389 | 0.680 | 0.538 | 0.500 | 0.542\n\nTimeout after 34201 updates (1805s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.642\n\nSeed 4 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.642\nTraining time: 1817s\nSUBMISSION_UUID: 613c9cda-a534-4a94-afa3-3cca34b5c7a4\nTotal time: 1826s\n============================================================\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 3 with timeout 30.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\
    \ | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -0.95 | 0.47 | 3.53 | 5.03 | 6.40 | 7.54 | 7.74 | 6.80\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.182 | 0.294 | 0.400 | 0.500 | 0.533 | 0.455\n\nTimeout after 34501 updates (1807s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.709\n\nSeed 3 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.709\nTraining time: 1820s\nFull training completed!\n\n\nSTDERR:\n2025-09-13 13:56:56,148\tINFO worker.py:1747 -- Connecting to existing Ray cluster at address: 10.60.151.21:6379... timestamp_ns=1757743016148975691\n2025-09-13 13:56:56,162\tINFO worker.py:1927 -- Connected to Ray cluster. timestamp_ns=1757743016162470522\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration\
    \ guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the\
