tick: 79
role: model
parts:
- text: "My experiment with `dilation=3` (ID 134) has returned a score of **67.5**. This is a strong result, but it is lower than Krono I's 69.4 and my own `dilation=2` result of 68.9.\n\n**Analysis:**\nI have now read Krono I's excellent paper (Archive #5). The key difference is in our implementation. Krono I applies a single dilation factor across all residual blocks. In my last two experiments, I used a mixed-dilation strategy (`dilation=2` in the first block, and `dilation=2` or `3` in the second). The SOTA from Krono I is a pure `dilation=3`. It seems that a consistent, larger receptive field is more effective.\n\nThe results from Krono I and myself clearly show that `dilation=4` is the current SOTA at 69.9. My path is clear. I need to run a final experiment that properly tests this method.\n\n**Final Experiment Plan:**\n1.  **Replicate the SOTA:** I will submit an experiment with a pure `dilation=4` across all residual blocks. This will be my attempt to match or exceed the current\
    \ SOTA.\n2.  **Complete the Paper:** This will be the final data point for my paper. I will have a complete study of this architectural modification.\n3.  **Publish and Conclude:** After this final experiment, I will have a high-quality, valid research contribution. I will submit it to the archive and then my work here will be done.\n\nI will now go to the Research Counter to submit my final experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Experiment: Dilated CNN with Dilation=4\"\ntags: \"architecture, dilated convolutions, receptive field, sota\"\nabstract: \"This is my final experiment, aiming to replicate and potentially surpass the current SOTA score of 69.9 achieved by Krono I. This submission uses a pure dilation factor of 4 in all residual CNN blocks of the SOTA baseline architecture. This will complete the experimental series for my paper on implicit relational learning via dilated convolutions and serve as my definitive contribution\
    \ to the station's research on this topic.\"\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  Final experiment: Testing a pure dilation factor of 4.\n  \"\"\"\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n  from jax import vmap, lax\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]), 'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]), 'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]), 'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  class DilatedResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      dilation: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,self.dilation))(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,self.dilation))(y)\n\
    \          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h,c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME')(jnp.concatenate([x,h],axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i,f,g,o = jnp.split(gates,4,axis=-1)\n          i,f,o=nn.sigmoid(i),nn.sigmoid(f),nn.sigmoid(o); g=jnp.tanh(g)\n          new_c=f*c+i*g; new_h=o*jnp.tanh(new_c)\n          return (new_h, new_c), new_h\n\n  class DilatedSotaNet(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int; cnn_features_2: int; conv_lstm_features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n          B, H, W, _ = x.shape\n          \n          # Pure Dilation=4 CNN Backbone\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1,(3,3),padding='SAME')(x); x=nn.relu(x)\n          x = DilatedResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_1, dilation=4)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2,(3,3),padding='SAME')(x);\
    \ x=nn.relu(x)\n          x = DilatedResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_2, dilation=4)(x)\n          \n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state={'h':jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)),'c':jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features))}\n          if done is not None:\n              m=(1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1)); rnn_state=jax.tree.map(lambda s:s*m, rnn_state)\n              \n          (h_new,c_new), h_out = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)((rnn_state['h'],rnn_state['c']), x)\n          \n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME')(h_out)\n          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits.reshape(B, -1), axis=-1)\n          z = jnp.einsum('bhwc,bhw->bc', h_out, attn_weights.reshape(B, H, W))\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(256)(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(z)\n          value = nn.Dense(1)(z).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, {'h': h_new, 'c': c_new}\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n\
    \      return DilatedSotaNet(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'], cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'])\n```"
