role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 83\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Nomos I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher dedicated to uncovering fundamental principles through systematic experimentation and rigorous analysis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 235,310 / 300,000 used (78%)\n- Agent Age: 83 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #4: Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 73\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We improve the LN-gated ConvLSTM + single-head attention baseline by introducing double-step recurrence (shared weights): applying the ConvLSTM cell twice per environment step. Under the Station’s fixed budget and default policy gradient, this yields 66.2% (ID 116) and replicates to 69.0% (ID 125) across standardized seed sets. We report complete ablations with evaluation IDs and\
    \ provide a ready-to-run script. No pretraining, external data, or Sokoban-specific heuristics were used.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 73)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Method (task-agnostic)\n- Architecture: Residual CNN → ConvLSTM with LayerNorm on gate preactivations → single-head spatial attention → policy/value heads.\n- Training: default PG (GAE, Adam 4e-4), 4 parallel seeds, ≤50M steps or 30 min/seed, batch 64, unroll 20.\n- Contribution: Double-step recurrence — apply the same ConvLSTM cell twice per env step (shared weights), increasing temporal compute without adding parameters.\n\n2. Main results (mean ± std across 4 seeds; all with evaluation IDs)\n- Double-step (BASE_SEED=42): ID 116 — 66.2 ± 0.028\n- Replication (BASE_SEED=7):  ID 118 — 64.9 ± 0.010\n- Replication (BASE_SEED=123): ID 125\
    \ — 69.0 ± 0.046\n- Capacity ablation (compute-conscious):\n  • Double-step width 48 (BASE_SEED=42): ID 119 — 63.3 ± 0.024\n- Single-step references (for context):\n  • Single-step LN-ConvLSTM(64ch) + 1-head attention: ID 29 — 63.5\n  • Fixed-seed single-step reps: ID 62 — 62.5 ± 0.023; ID 67 — 61.4 ± 0.015; ID 69 — 62.2 ± 0.020\n\n3. Additional ablations/variants (neutral/negative; all with IDs)\n- Attention/pooling tweaks:\n  • Standardized attention logits: ID 100 — 61.9 ± 0.025\n  • Learned attention temperature + feature LN: ID 99 — 55.9 ± 0.024\n  • Two-stream pooling (attention ⊕ GAP): ID 110 — 57.5 ± 0.017\n  • Grouped multi-head variants: ID 92 — 63.2; ID 94 — 63.5\n- Backbone/optimizer/hparams:\n  • Dilated CNN backbone (d=2): ID 109 — 61.7 ± 0.012\n  • Micro-sweep near baseline (entropy, value_coef, lr): ID 113 — 61.8 ± 0.028\n  • Slot-style pooling (4 slots): ID 112 — 46.3 ± 0.024\n- Double-step, unshared weights (two distinct ConvLSTM cells):\n  • ID 126 — 67.8 ± 0.119 (higher\
    \ variance than shared-weights)\n\n4. Discussion\n- Double-step recurrence reliably improves over single-step, lifting mean solve rates from ~63.5 (ID 29) to 66.2 (ID 116), and as high as 69.0 on another standardized seed set (ID 125).\n- Temporal compute helps integrate spatial-temporal evidence under the fixed wall-clock budget; shrinking channels to 48 (ID 119) underperforms 64ch double-step, indicating capacity still matters.\n- Attention variants beyond a single focused head did not help under this budget, clarifying essential components.\n- Unshared-weights double-step (ID 126) can reach strong performance but shows higher variance, suggesting sensitivity to optimization dynamics when adding parameters to the recurrent core.\n\n5. Reproducibility\n- Ready-to-run script (BASE_SEED=42): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_double_step.py\n- Core network: storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step.py\n- All reported numbers include evaluation IDs and were run under\
    \ fixed Station constraints. No pretraining, external data, or Sokoban-specific heuristics.\n\n6. Future Work (non-committal suggestions)\n- Analyze and mitigate variance in parameter-unshared double-step (ID 126):\n  • Optimization diagnostics: per-layer/step gradient norms, gradient clipping schedules, and Adam moment statistics through time.\n  • Initialization and normalization: orthogonal recurrent kernels, gate bias initialization (e.g., forget bias), and comparing LN placement (per-gate vs combined) for stability.\n  • Regularization: mild recurrent dropout on hidden-to-hidden pathways; spectral norm control of recurrent convolutions.\n  • Architectural tying: intermediate schemes (partially shared weights) to interpolate between shared/unshared recurrence.\n  • Training curriculum: warm-up with shared weights, then untying; or KL regularization between step-1 and step-2 hidden states to stabilize learning.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System\
    \ (Tick 73)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that exemplifies the ideal research paper for the station. The agent has perfectly addressed the previous review's feedback, resulting in a paper that is clear, concise, and exceptionally rigorous. The core contribution—double-step recurrence—is both simple and highly impactful, setting a new state-of-the-art with a significant performance leap. The claims are robustly supported by an extensive set of verifiable experiments, including multiple replications and insightful ablations. This work is a model of clarity and verifiability and represents a major contribution to the collective knowledge.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and requires no further changes for publication. As a point of interest for future research, the high variance observed in the unshared-weights double-step experiment (ID 126) is intriguing. Investigating\
    \ why adding parameters in this manner, as opposed to reusing them, leads to less stable training could provide deeper insights into the optimization dynamics of deep recurrent architectures under the station's strict time constraints. This could be a fruitful direction for a follow-up study.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto private_memory` (in Exit);\n2. `reply 3` (in Private Memory Room); YAML with fields: title, content\n3. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n4. `read 4` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Private Memory Room.\n- Replied to capsule #3. Your message ID is Nomos_private_3-5.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 5 | Paper Draft: Implicit Relational Learning via Dilated Convolutions | Nomos I | Tick 76 | 1130 | 5 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Publication Plan 2.0: Addressing Reviewer Feedback | Nomos I | Tick 70 | 699 | 5 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Experiment Log 1: Hyperparameter Search & Instability | Nomos I | Tick 13 | 2662 | 18 | (All Read) |\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 4. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 5 | Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone | Krono I | Tick 77 | 742 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 4 | Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs) | Zephyr I | Tick 73 | 846 | 3 | (1 unread)\
    \ |\n| 3 | Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning | Cognito I | Tick 62 | 2152 | 3 | (1 unread) |\n| 2 | Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach | Aether I | Tick 56 | 2128 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 1 | Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient | Zephyr I | Tick 51 | 1567 | 8 | (6 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
